//! A scope for spawned tasks: every task spawned into a `TaskGroup` completes
//! before the group is destroyed. The default group runs its tasks
//! cooperatively on the current thread; `TaskGroup(threads: n)` with `n` of two
//! or more runs them on a pool of `n` worker threads. Tasks share data through
//! `Arc`, `Mutex` or `Channel`. A task in a group created with `TaskGroup()`
//! may also borrow from the frame that spawned it. Any `threads:` spelling
//! opts the group into `Send` checking whatever its value, and a reference is
//! not `Send`, so such a group refuses that borrow.

// TaskGroup: the structured-concurrency nursery.
//
// A `TaskGroup` is a local. `group.spawn(f(args))` is compiler-lowered: the
// coro transform turns `f` into a frame with a `Resumable` conformance, and the
// synthesized `__spawn_f` helper builds the frame, boxes it erased
// (`Box<any Resumable>`), enqueues it here, and returns a typed `Task<T>`. The
// group's `Deinit` runs every child to completion (structured join = LIFO
// destruction), with no forced destroy.
//
// The parallel vectors are the run queue and the scheduler bookkeeping:
//   * `tasks`     — the erased frames (`resume`/`wake_reason` dispatch here).
//                   `None` once the task completes, because the frame box is
//                   released at completion, and also while an MT worker is
//                   resuming the frame (see the claim below).
//   * `cells`     — the per-task result/cancel cell, which outlives the frame:
//                   `Task` points here, never into the frame.
//   * `done`      — per-task completion; a done frame is never resumed again
//                   (its dispatch loop is guarded, but re-resuming would
//                   busy-spin).
//   * `remaining` — a copy of the frame's wake word: nanoseconds until the task
//                   should next resume (0 = ready now, > 0 = sleeping), or a
//                   park state (the park word vocabulary below). Relative
//                   sleeps, so no wall clock is needed. Nanoseconds are the
//                   unit `Duration` and `sleep` use; the reactor poll's
//                   millisecond timeout is converted at one boundary
//                   (`__saw_exec_poll_ms`).
//
// Two engines. The default `TaskGroup()` is cooperative and single-threaded:
// tasks are stackless coroutine frames driven on the current thread.
// `Thread.spawn { ... }` (one OS thread per body) is a separate engine and
// shares no scheduler with it.
//
// Multi-threaded mode: `TaskGroup(threads: N)` (N >= 2) runs one
// mutex-protected shared run queue (one injector, N workers: simplicity over
// per-worker lock-free deques) served by N OS worker threads. The pool starts
// at the group's first spawn and lives until the group's `Deinit`, so an MT
// group's tasks run concurrently with the thread that owns the group
// (design 225).
//
// The locking discipline, which is what makes that safe:
//
//   * One mutex (`lock`) guards every scheduling read and write of the group's
//     state: `tasks`, `cells`, `done`, `remaining`, `active`, the slot identity
//     trio `gen`/`free`/`pin`, and `started`/`stopping`. It is taken by
//     `count`, `__enqueue` (the spawning thread), the handle surface
//     (`__stale_sync`/`__recycle_sync`/`__pin_sync`/`__detach_slot`/
//     `__await_slot`), `__shutdown_crew` and `__saw_exec_worker`. The
//     diagnostic walks (the task dump and the quiescent check) read without
//     it, and say so. The synthesized spawn helper also reads `__gen_at`
//     without it, which is a race on an MT group (see the slot-identity
//     trio; SL-392).
//   * A claim moves the frame out of its slot. Under the lock a worker sets
//     `active` and `swap_out`s the frame box into a local, resumes it with the
//     lock released, then puts it back and records the outcome under the lock
//     again. So no borrow of a run-queue element is outstanding across an
//     unlocked region, which is what lets the queue grow while workers run: a
//     `push` may reallocate the vector's buffer and nothing points into it. It
//     also makes one-thread-at-a-time confinement a fact about ownership: while
//     a frame runs, its slot holds `None` and the running worker owns the box.
//   * An MT group is enqueued into by its owner only. A single-threaded group's
//     task may be handed its own group as a `&TaskGroup` and spawn siblings
//     onto it; an MT group's cannot, because a reference is not `Send` and the
//     Send gate refuses it at the spawn (conformance row K19). So `__enqueue`
//     has one thread. That is why `started` needs no CAS and the crew vector
//     needs no lock, but it is not why the queue is safe: `free`/`gen` have a
//     second writer in any worker that completes a task.
//
// Two assumptions do not hold for an MT group, and code may lean on them by
// mistake: the run queue does resize while workers run (the owner spawns at
// any time; claim-by-move is what makes that safe), and the slot identity
// vectors do have concurrent writers (a worker completing a `Void` task
// recycles its slot), which is why they are under the lock.
//
// Idle workers park on a condition variable beside the run-queue mutex rather
// than spinning: `__enqueue` broadcasts after every push, and every resume
// broadcasts its outcome, so a worker sleeps at no cost until there is work or
// the group is shutting down.
//
// `join` and `Deinit` on an MT group:
//
//   * `handle.join()` waits for that one task and returns its result. It drives
//     nothing (the pool is already running the task): it waits on the
//     condition variable the workers broadcast on, and the pool is untouched
//     when it returns. That is what `join` means on the single-threaded engine
//     too.
//   * The workers persist across joins, so a group's thread cost does not grow
//     with how often it is joined.
//   * `Deinit` signals no-more-work, joins the workers, then runs the eager
//     teardown. It is a structured join: `stopping` cancels nothing, and a
//     worker leaves only once nothing is live.
//   * The scope a group defines is its ownership extent, not a lexical block:
//     a heap-owned group's pool starts at its first spawn and is joined
//     wherever its last owner is released. Nothing here reads block structure.

extern "C" {
    // The pthread mutex (the init seam is rt/common/pthread.saw; the rest
    // resolve from libSystem/libc). std/channel.saw uses the same symbols.
    func __saw_rt_pthread_mutex_init_default(m: UnsafePointer<Int8>)
    func pthread_mutex_lock(m: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int
    func pthread_mutex_unlock(m: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int
    func pthread_mutex_destroy(m: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int

    // The idle worker's park. Same seam std/channel.saw uses for its blocking
    // `recv`, declared here because each std file is its own module.
    // `broadcast` rather than `signal`: one push can make several parked
    // workers runnable, and waking one too many costs a re-scan while waking
    // one too few costs a worker.
    func __saw_rt_pthread_cond_init_default(c: UnsafePointer<Int8>)
    func pthread_cond_wait(c: UnsafePointer<Int8>, m: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int
    func pthread_cond_broadcast(c: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int
    func pthread_cond_destroy(c: UnsafePointer<Int8>) -> Int

    // The instance-based IO reactor seams (kqueue on macOS, epoll on Linux;
    // bodies in sawc/rt/host_*/reactor.saw). All but `create` take the reactor
    // instance `r` that `__saw_rt_reactor_create` returned; the process-wide
    // instance is `__saw_host_reactor()` below, and `SystemReactor` wraps these
    // seams as the hosted `Reactor` impl.
    func __saw_rt_reactor_create() -> Int
    func __saw_rt_reactor_destroy(r: Int)
    // Arm one-shot readiness on `fd` in `r` (write==0 => read). `token` is the
    // parked task's ROOT frame `__wake`-word address (a nested frame hands
    // that token down), or 0 from outside any frame.
    func __saw_rt_reactor_register(r: Int, fd: Int, write: Int, token: Int)
    // Drop an armed registration that never fired, so a token pointing into a
    // frame cannot outlive the frame. Idempotent: unregistering an
    // already-fired one-shot or an unarmed fd is a no-op.
    func __saw_rt_reactor_unregister(r: Int, fd: Int, write: Int)
    // Block up to `timeout_ms` (< 0 = forever); latch each ready token; return
    // the number of events delivered, a consumed wake event included.
    func __saw_rt_reactor_poll(r: Int, timeout_ms: Int) -> Int
    // Rouse every thread blocked in `poll` on `r`, from any thread: `cancel()`
    // uses it so an io-parked task's poll returns and the scheduler re-checks
    // its cancel flag, and MT workers use it to reach a scheduler parked on
    // another thread.
    func __saw_rt_reactor_wake(r: Int)

    // The host timer seam. `__saw_exec_sleep_ns` below wraps it as the
    // executor's own blocking sleep, for the parks that never need cutting
    // short. Nanoseconds, read as unsigned; the body chunks to libc's bound.
    func __saw_rt_sleep_ns(ns: UInt64)

    // The monotonic clock: a park that a reactor wake cut short charges its
    // sleepers what actually elapsed, and an io park's deadline is an absolute
    // instant on it. Declared here as well as in std.time because each std
    // file is its own module.
    func __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() -> Int64
}

// =============================================================================
// The Reactor trait
// =============================================================================
// Every register, unregister, poll and wake the executor makes goes through
// this trait. It is token-based: the token is the parked task's root-frame
// `__wake`-word address, latched to 0 when its (fd, direction) fires (design
// 91); nested frames inherit that token and report their own reasons upward. The hosted
// impl `SystemReactor` wraps the `__saw_rt_reactor_*` instance seams; an
// SOS-hosted runtime would implement the same trait over its own syscalls. Internal
// to the executor module (not `public`), so no user-facing `Reactor` or
// `SystemReactor` name can collide with it.
trait Reactor {
    func register(&self, fd: Int, dir: Int, token: Int)
    // The inverse of `register`. A park that ends without its event firing
    // must drop the registration, or the token (an address inside the frame)
    // outlives the frame that owns it.
    func unregister(&self, fd: Int, dir: Int)
    func poll(&self, timeout_ms: Int) -> Int
    func wake(&self)
}

// The hosted reactor: a value wrapping the process-wide reactor instance (the
// opaque pointer from `__saw_rt_reactor_create`). kqueue vs epoll lives in the
// rt/ bodies behind the seams, so one wrapper serves both hosts. Used through
// static dispatch rather than an `any Reactor` existential: the implementation
// is chosen at link time, so boxing would cost an allocation and buy nothing.
struct SystemReactor {
    instance: Int
}

extension SystemReactor: Reactor {
    func register(&self, fd: Int, dir: Int, token: Int) {
        __saw_rt_reactor_register(self.instance, fd, dir, token)
    }
    func unregister(&self, fd: Int, dir: Int) {
        __saw_rt_reactor_unregister(self.instance, fd, dir)
    }
    func poll(&self, timeout_ms: Int) -> Int {
        __saw_rt_reactor_poll(self.instance, timeout_ms)
    }
    func wake(&self) {
        __saw_rt_reactor_wake(self.instance)
    }
}

// The process-wide reactor instance, created on first use and published by
// CAS: a thread that loses the race destroys its spare and uses the winner's.
// The instance lives for the whole process and is never destroyed.
static __saw_reactor_instance: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

func __saw_host_reactor() -> SystemReactor {
    let cur = __saw_reactor_instance.load()
    if cur != 0 {
        return SystemReactor(instance: cur)
    }
    let newp = __saw_rt_reactor_create()
    if __saw_reactor_instance.compare_exchange(0, newp) {
        return SystemReactor(instance: newp)
    }
    __saw_rt_reactor_destroy(newp)
    SystemReactor(instance: __saw_reactor_instance.load())
}

static NANOS_PER_MILLI: Int = 1000000
// The back-off nap (an MT worker whose peer is mid-resume, and the quiescent
// check's confirmation), and the MT worker's io poll cap.
static BACKOFF_NANOS: Int = 1000000
static MT_POLL_CAP_NANOS: Int = 50000000

// =============================================================================
// The park word vocabulary (design 230)
// =============================================================================
// A frame's `__wake`, and its slot's `remaining` (a copy of it), is one Int
// carrying four states. The two negative states are decoded only by these
// predicates (and by `__park_flag_set`, which decodes the address); the
// encoders are `Channel.__park_word` and the transform's `IO_PARK_WAKE`. Every
// scan asks through the predicates, so a new park state is added here.
//
//    > 0    a sleep deadline, in nanoseconds
//   == 0    ready: resume on the next sweep (`yield_now`, a budget yield)
//   == -1   the io park: the reactor holds a registration whose readiness
//           event latches this frame's `__wake` word back to 0
//    < -1   a park on a readiness word: the negated address of an `Int`
//           somebody else owns, and the frame resumes once that word is
//           nonzero. A readiness word is 8-aligned and never at address 0, so
//           `-1` can never name one and the two negative states cannot
//           collide. `Channel` uses it (`Channel.__park_word`). The executor
//           knows only "resume when the word this frame named is nonzero", so
//           it needs no channel import, layout knowledge or per-channel
//           bookkeeping, and a send wakes only the receivers parked on that
//           channel, since no other frame's wake word names that address.
//
// A deadline-bounded io park is a fifth state, carried as a pair rather than
// a fifth number: see the park-deadline funnel below.
static WAKE_IO: Int = -1

func __park_is_io(wake: Int) sync -> Bool { wake == WAKE_IO }

func __park_is_flag(wake: Int) sync -> Bool { wake < WAKE_IO }

// =============================================================================
// The park-deadline funnel (design 272)
// =============================================================================
// An io park can carry a deadline: `io_wait_until(fd, dir, at)` arms the
// reactor as `io_wait` does and also stamps the frame's `__io_deadline` field,
// which `Resumable.io_deadline()` reads. The state is the pair, not a fifth
// number in the wake word:
//
//     __wake == -1  &&  io_deadline() == 0   the io park
//     __wake == -1  &&  io_deadline() >  0   the deadline-bounded io park
//
// The deadline is an absolute monotonic nanosecond instant, never a countdown,
// and that matters: the `remaining` bookkeeping beside it is relative and is
// decremented by whichever thread just polled, so two MT workers polling one
// group would charge a countdown twice and time a frame out early.
// `now >= deadline` is idempotent (every reader on every thread computes the
// same answer from the same clock), so the MT path needs no accounting.
//
// `__park_deadline_remaining` and `__park_deadline_expired` are the only place
// the executor interprets the deadline half of the vocabulary. ENTRY POINTS,
// all in this file:
//
//   1. `__saw_exec_run_inner`, phase 2 (the ambient sweep's poll bound)
//   2. `__saw_exec_wake_io` (the ambient sweep's post-poll promote)
//   3. `__saw_exec_worker`, io branch (the MT worker's bound and promote)
//   4. `__saw_exec_park_io` (the single-frame drive's io park)
//
// A new reader calls these; it never re-spells `now >= deadline`.

// What is left of a deadline, as the relative nanosecond span the poll-bound
// arithmetic speaks. `-1` means "this frame has no deadline", the same "no
// bound" value a group with no sleeper contributes, so a deadline drops into
// the earliest-deadline fold with no new cases. A deadline already past
// answers 0 rather than a negative span: the frame is runnable now, and a
// negative would read as "block indefinitely" one layer up.
func __park_deadline_remaining(deadline: Int, now: Int) sync -> Int {
    if deadline <= 0 {
        return -1
    }
    if now >= deadline {
        return 0
    }
    deadline - now
}

// Has this frame's deadline arrived? A frame with no deadline never expires.
func __park_deadline_expired(deadline: Int, now: Int) sync -> Bool {
    deadline > 0 && now >= deadline
}

// Why an io-parked frame becomes runnable, in one place: the ambient sweep and
// the MT worker both promote io parks, and asking this keeps their tests from
// drifting apart.
//
//   * the reactor latched the frame's wake word: the event it registered for
//     has fired;
//   * a peer cancelled it: it resumes, re-checks `cancelled()` at its
//     park-loop top and bails;
//   * its own deadline arrived: it resumes, re-issues its syscall, and reports
//     the timeout itself.
//
// All three mean the same thing to the scheduler ("make this slot runnable")
// and different things to the frame, which is exactly why the frame decides
// which happened and this does not try to tell it.
func __park_io_runnable(wake: Int, cancelled: Bool, deadline: Int, now: Int) sync -> Bool {
    wake >= 0 || cancelled || __park_deadline_expired(deadline, now)
}

// Fold one frame's deadline into a scan's running earliest-deadline value.
// `best` and the result are both "-1 = no bound", so this is what every entry
// point above calls instead of open-coding the comparison.
func __park_deadline_fold(best: Int, deadline: Int, now: Int) sync -> Int {
    let rem = __park_deadline_remaining(deadline, now)
    if rem < 0 {
        return best
    }
    if best < 0 || rem < best {
        return rem
    }
    best
}

// Read the readiness word a `__park_is_flag` wake reason names. Unsynchronized
// by design: the word is written under its owner's own lock, a stale false
// costs one more re-scan (every park on a readiness word is bounded), and a
// stale true costs one resume that finds nothing and re-parks. Neither can be
// wrong for long, and neither can hang.
func __park_flag_set(wake: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    let flag = (0 - wake) as UnsafePointer<Int>
    flag[0] != 0
}

// The re-scan bound for a park on a readiness word. Short, because it is the
// fallback a cross-thread handoff lands on when the sender's poke misses;
// bounded at all, because the word's writer may be on another thread and no
// engine may block on it forever.
static FLAG_POLL_NANOS: Int = 1000000

// Cap a park's timeout while a readiness-word park is live. The word may have a
// writer on another thread, so no engine may block on anything longer than the
// re-scan bound while one exists: "block indefinitely" (-1) becomes the bound,
// a longer deadline is shortened to it, and a shorter one is left alone. With
// no such park in play the deadline passes through untouched.
func __saw_exec_flag_bound(deadline: Int, flag: Bool) sync -> Int {
    if not flag {
        return deadline
    }
    if deadline < 0 || deadline > FLAG_POLL_NANOS {
        return FLAG_POLL_NANOS
    }
    deadline
}

// How many `Thread.spawn {}` thread-engine bodies might still be running. The
// quiescent report needs it, because a thread-engine body is code this process
// can still run and no group's run queue knows about one (K56 is that program:
// main parks on a channel a `Thread.spawn {}` body will feed).
//
// Bumped by spawn codegen at the launch and dropped by `join` and by `detach`
// on `Thread`/`VoidThread` (std/task.saw). A joined body has finished. A
// detached body may still run, but it stops being counted at `detach`, so the
// report does not wait for it. A finished but unjoined thread keeps the count
// up, which can only suppress a report.
static __saw_exec_thread_live: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

public func __saw_exec_thread_task_started() {
    let _ = __saw_exec_thread_live.fetch_add(1)
}

public func __saw_exec_thread_task_ended() {
    let _ = __saw_exec_thread_live.fetch_add(-1)
}

// How many owner threads are currently inside the executor, unable to run any
// code of their own.
//
// This is what the frame walk cannot see. Worker threads are accounted by the
// walk (a mid-resume worker sets its slot's `active`, an idle one has nothing
// to run), and thread-engine bodies are counted above. What is left is the
// owner thread, which created the groups and runs ordinary program code
// between its calls into the executor, and nothing about a run queue reveals
// whether it is computing or waiting. An MT worker that reported a deadlock
// while the owner was mid-computation would be aborting a correct program,
// which is worse than the hang it replaces.
//
// A depth counter rather than a flag, because these nest: an outer sweep
// drives a task that joins another. Entered by `__saw_exec_run` (the ambient
// sweep), `__saw_exec_park_flag` (the single-frame drive), `__await_slot` (an
// MT join) and `__shutdown_crew` (an MT `Deinit`): every place an owner thread
// hands itself to the executor and cannot come back until the executor lets
// it. A second owner thread can only be a thread-engine body, which blocks the
// report on its own while it is counted above, so one counter is enough.
static __saw_exec_in_executor: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

func __saw_exec_enter() {
    let _ = __saw_exec_in_executor.fetch_add(1)
}

func __saw_exec_leave() {
    let _ = __saw_exec_in_executor.fetch_add(-1)
}

// The publishing side's poke, called from `Channel._enqueue` once a readiness
// word is published, and from `close`. A receiver parked on that word is
// resumable the instant it is written, but an engine blocked elsewhere will not
// look until whatever it is blocked on returns; every park on a readiness word
// is a bounded reactor poll, and this ends one early. Never required for
// correctness: a poke that does not land costs latency, not a wake. It pokes
// only a reactor that already exists, so a program doing no io pays neither an
// fd nor an allocation for its channel traffic.
public func __saw_exec_chan_wake() {
    if __saw_reactor_instance.load() != 0 {
        __saw_host_reactor().wake()
    }
}

// The executor's blocking sleep over the `__saw_rt_sleep_ns` seam. Not a
// suspension source: it blocks the OS thread, and nothing can cut the wait
// short. So it serves only parks that never need abandoning (the single-frame
// drive's timer park and the back-off naps); every other park is a reactor
// poll, which a wake can end.
func __saw_exec_sleep_ns(ns: Int) {
    if ns > 0 {
        __saw_rt_sleep_ns(ns as UInt64)
    }
}

// A nanosecond deadline as the reactor poll's millisecond timeout, rounded up.
// A sleep is a floor, and when a poll times out the io branches charge each
// sleeper the whole requested span, so the poll must have waited at least that
// long; rounding down would wake a sleeper early (and turn a sub-millisecond
// span into a zero-timeout poll). A negative deadline (nothing is sleeping)
// passes through as the reactor's "block indefinitely".
func __saw_exec_poll_ms(ns: Int) -> Int {
    if ns < 0 {
        return -1
    }
    (ns + NANOS_PER_MILLI - 1) / NANOS_PER_MILLI
}

// The single-frame drive's park policy, so the synthesized entry executor
// emits no park body: only `resume` and this one call. Given a frame's
// `__wake` reason: a positive value is a sleep deadline in nanoseconds (there
// is no other task or timer to honour in a single-frame drive, so park the
// thread that long); -1 is an io park; a readiness-word park waits on the
// word; zero is a plain yield (resume at once).
//
// The timer park stays on the sleep seam rather than a reactor poll. It is
// reached only from the entry executor of a suspending `main` with no spawns
// (a `sleep` outside any frame lowers to the seam directly): there is no
// sibling to cede to, no TaskGroup to reach a `cancel()` through, and no
// `remaining` bookkeeping to re-park against if a poll came back early. A
// pokeable park would have nothing to be poked by.
//
// Besides the entry executor, codegen reaches this from an `io_wait` or a
// channel park outside any frame. `deadline` is the frame's `__io_deadline`
// (0 = none): the entry executor reads the field directly, since it holds the
// concrete frame, and the codegen fallbacks pass 0 for a channel park and an
// untimed `io_wait`, and the deadline for `io_wait_until`.
func __saw_exec_park(wake: Int, deadline: Int) {
    if wake > 0 {
        __saw_exec_sleep_ns(wake)
    } else if __park_is_io(wake) {
        __saw_exec_park_io(deadline)
    } else if __park_is_flag(wake) {
        __saw_exec_park_flag(wake)
    }
}

// Entry point 4 of the park-deadline funnel: the single-frame drive's io park.
// Without a deadline it polls with no timeout until an event arrives, since a
// single-frame drive has nothing else to honour. With one, the poll is bounded
// by what is left of it, so the drive loop comes back round and resumes the
// frame, which re-issues its syscall and decides for itself whether that was
// readiness or a timeout.
//
// An already-expired deadline polls with a zero timeout rather than skipping the
// poll: a reactor event that arrived in the same instant is still worth
// latching, and a zero-timeout poll cannot block.
func __saw_exec_park_io(deadline: Int) {
    if deadline <= 0 {
        let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(-1)
        return
    }
    let now = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() as Int
    let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(
        __saw_exec_poll_ms(__park_deadline_remaining(deadline, now)))
}

// The single-frame drive's park on a readiness word. Reached when a suspending
// `main` with no spawns parks on a channel, and when a channel receive runs
// outside any frame.
//
// There is no sibling to cede to on this path, so the frame's park is the
// thread's: wait until the word its wake reason names is nonzero. The wait is
// a reactor poll rather than a timer nap, so a `__saw_exec_chan_wake` poke from
// whichever thread publishes the word ends it at once; it is bounded, so a
// poke that lands before the poll begins cannot be lost. The writer here is
// always another thread (an MT worker or a `Thread.spawn {}` body), because a
// cooperative sender would be a second frame and this drive has one.
func __saw_exec_park_flag(wake: Int) unsafe {
    // This thread hands itself to the executor for the length of the park and
    // can run nothing of its own until the word is set.
    __saw_exec_enter()
    __saw_exec_park_flag_loop(wake)
    __saw_exec_leave()
}

func __saw_exec_park_flag_loop(wake: Int) unsafe {
    while not __park_flag_set(wake) {
        // This drive's own frame is on no run queue, so the quiescent walk
        // cannot see it: `true` tells the walk that the caller is itself a
        // parked frame.
        __saw_exec_check_quiescent(true)
        let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(__saw_exec_poll_ms(FLAG_POLL_NANOS))
    }
}

// The reactor-arm entry point that the coroutine transform's `io_wait` and
// offload park lowerings, and the outside-frame `io_wait` codegen path, call.
// Synthesized IR cannot do trait dispatch, so the arm goes through this Saw
// function to reach the `Reactor` trait. `dir` is the direction word
// (0 = read); `token` is the parked task's root-frame `__wake`-word address,
// or 0 from outside any frame. A failed arm is not reported (SL-354).
func __saw_exec_io_register(fd: Int, dir: Int, token: Int) {
    __saw_host_reactor().register(fd, dir, token)
}

// The disarm entry point; `io_unwait` lowers to it. std's park loops call it
// when they leave without their event firing (a cancellation or a timeout),
// and a coroutine frame's synthesized `release` calls it for the last fd the
// frame armed, covering any exit a library loop missed. Safe when nothing is
// armed: the seam is idempotent.
func __saw_exec_io_unregister(fd: Int, dir: Int) {
    __saw_host_reactor().unregister(fd, dir)
}

// Rouse every blocked reactor poll: `cancel()` uses it so an io-parked target
// observes the cancel now, and MT workers use it to reach a scheduler parked
// on another thread.
func __saw_exec_reactor_wake() {
    __saw_host_reactor().wake()
}

// The ambient cooperative scheduler (design 89): one process-wide intrusive
// list of every live single-threaded TaskGroup, threaded through each group's
// `next` field; `__saw_exec_head` holds the head group's address (0 = empty).
// Every drive point (the ambient entry executor, `Task.join`/`VoidTask.join`,
// a group's `Deinit`, `__saw_bg_close`) sweeps this one list through
// `__saw_exec_run`, so a task that parks (sleep / io / channel) cedes to every
// sibling across all groups, not just its own, and a spawn runs whenever the
// scheduler runs, not only at join. A TaskGroup is a membership and lifetime
// scope over this shared scheduler: its Deinit drives the list until its own
// members are done (structured join), then drops only its own boxes. Nested
// groups link into the same list. MT groups (workers >= 2) keep their own
// queue and worker pool and never join this list.
static __saw_exec_head: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

// A second intrusive list, which every group joins on its first spawn,
// single-threaded and multi-threaded alike, for the task dump and the
// quiescent deadlock check. The scheduler list above must exclude MT groups
// (the ambient sweep must never resume a frame an MT worker owns), but those
// two walks must see every live task, so each group carries two links, one
// per list. Nothing schedules from this list.
static __saw_bt_head: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

// Set once, before the first byte of a panic-time dump, and never cleared:
// the process is ending either way. It is what stops a panic raised by the
// walk from re-entering the walk that raised it.
static __saw_bt_dumping: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

// =============================================================================
// The per-task cell (design 134)
// =============================================================================
// A spawned task's result and cancel word live here, beside the slot and owned
// by the group, not inside the coroutine frame. That is what lets the frame box
// be released the moment the task completes: a `Task` addresses the cell and
// never the frame, and `cancel_addr()` hands a peer an address that outlives
// the task it cancels.
//
// The cell is typed (it holds a `T?`), so the group holds it erased and lets
// the box teardown run the right destructor; the group never names `T`.
// `__carries_result` is the one thing the erased side must know: a cell with a
// result slot has to survive its task (until `join` takes the value, or until
// group teardown drops it exactly once), while a `Void` task's cell holds
// nothing joinable, so its slot is reclaimable the moment the task completes.
// `public` only because the coroutine transform names these three types when
// it lowers a spawn, so their identities must stay the plain spellings
// `sawc/coro_transform.py` writes.
//
// Two more words live in the cell for the same reason the cancel word does: a
// handle must still read them after its task has finished (design 242).
//   `__fated`    — has this handle's fate (`join`/`detach`/`cancel`) been
//                  written? The handle's deinit panic reads it through a raw
//                  pointer from a `&self` method, as `cancel` writes the cancel
//                  word. Only a `Task.spawn` handle consults it: the check is
//                  gated on the handle's provenance, not its type.
//   `__detached` — the task keeps running, and its result is dropped at
//                  completion instead of being kept for a `join` that will
//                  never come.
// `__cancel_now` lets `__saw_bg_close` cancel every live member of the
// background group through the erased box, and `__drop_result` drops a
// detached task's result. Both need the typed impl (only `__ResultCell<T>`
// knows what a result is), which is what the vtable is for.
public trait __TaskCell {
    func __carries_result(&self) sync -> Bool
    func __is_detached(&self) sync -> Bool
    func __cancel_now(&var self) sync
    func __detach_now(&var self) sync
    func __drop_result(&var self) sync
}

// The cell for a task whose body returns a value. `__result` is the slot
// `Task<T>.result_ptr` addresses and `join` empties with `Optional.take`.
public struct __ResultCell<T> {
    private __result: T?
    private __cancel: Bool
    private __fated: Bool
    private __detached: Bool
}

extension __ResultCell<T>: NoCopy {}

extension __ResultCell<T>: __TaskCell {
    func __carries_result(&self) sync -> Bool { true }
    func __is_detached(&self) sync -> Bool { self.__detached }
    func __cancel_now(&var self) sync { self.__cancel = true }
    func __detach_now(&var self) sync { self.__detached = true }
    // The one place the erased side reaches a typed result. `take()` leaves
    // `None` behind, so the value drops here, exactly once, and the cell's own
    // later teardown drops nothing.
    func __drop_result(&var self) sync { let _ = self.__result.take() }
}

// The cell for a `Void` task and for the ambient scheduler's `Void` root frame:
// a cancel word and the handle words, and no value that outlives the task.
public struct __VoidCell {
    private __cancel: Bool
    private __fated: Bool
    private __detached: Bool
}

extension __VoidCell: __TaskCell {
    func __carries_result(&self) sync -> Bool { false }
    func __is_detached(&self) sync -> Bool { self.__detached }
    func __cancel_now(&var self) sync { self.__cancel = true }
    func __detach_now(&var self) sync { self.__detached = true }
    func __drop_result(&var self) sync {}
}

public struct TaskGroup {
    private tasks: Vector<Box<any Resumable>?>
    // The per-task cells, parallel to `tasks`. A slot's cell outlives its
    // frame: it holds the result until `join` takes it (or until teardown) and
    // the cancel word a stale handle may still address.
    private cells: Vector<Box<any __TaskCell>?>
    private done: Vector<Bool>
    private remaining: Vector<Int>
    // Per-task "this frame is being resumed right now". On an MT group it keeps
    // two workers from resuming one frame (set at claim and cleared at the
    // outcome, both under the lock); on the single-threaded engine it is what
    // lets a nested sweep skip a frame that is live on the C stack.
    private active: Vector<Bool>
    // The slot free-list and its generation counters (design 134). A slot with
    // nothing left to hold goes on `free` for the next `spawn` to claim, and its
    // generation retires, so a handle to the task that used to live there is
    // recognisably stale rather than silently addressing its successor. That is
    // what makes reuse safe: an index alone is ambiguous once reused, and the
    // generation makes (index, generation) a handle. `pin` marks a slot whose
    // raw cancel address escaped through `cancel_addr()`: that address has to
    // outlive the task, so the slot keeps its cell and is never handed out
    // again.
    private gen: Vector<Int>
    private free: Vector<Int>
    private pin: Vector<Bool>
    // The task dump's view of a slot's frame, recorded at enqueue and cleared
    // at completion: the frame type's backtrace-table index (`Resumable.bt_desc`,
    // -1 when the binary carries no table) and the frame box's payload address
    // (0 when the slot holds nothing).
    //
    // The dump cannot read these out of `tasks`: a worker claims a frame by
    // moving its box out of the slot, so a running task (the state a dump most
    // needs to show, and the one the dumping task is itself in) has no box
    // there. Recording them here also keeps the dump's unsynchronized reads off
    // the box handles: a box handle is two words and a torn read of one is a
    // wild pointer, while these are single words that are correct or absent.
    // Constant per task (a box does not move and a frame does not change type),
    // so there is nothing to keep up to date between the two writes.
    private frame_desc: Vector<Int>
    private frame_addr: Vector<Int>
    // Requested worker count. 0 or 1 is the single-threaded engine; 2 or more
    // is the worker pool (a mutex-protected shared queue served by `workers`
    // OS threads).
    private workers: Int
    // The run-queue mutex, in a conservative 64-byte slot (`pthread_mutex_t` is
    // at most 64 bytes on the hosted targets). `None` for the single-threaded
    // engine, which takes no lock.
    private lock: UnsafePointer<Int8>?
    // The idle workers' condition variable, in the same allocation as the mutex
    // (at +64), because the two are one object: a condvar without its mutex is
    // meaningless, and a separate allocation would be a second way for one to
    // exist without the other. `None` exactly when `lock` is.
    private cond: UnsafePointer<Int8>?
    // Has the pool been started, and has it been told to stop? Both are set
    // under the run-queue lock. `started` flips at the first spawn (so a group
    // that never spawns costs no threads); `stopping` flips once, in `Deinit`,
    // and a worker exits only when it is set and no live task is left, which
    // is what keeps teardown a structured join.
    private started: Bool
    private stopping: Bool
    // The worker pool's thread handles, joined at `Deinit`. Touched only by the
    // owning thread (start and teardown), so no lock.
    private crew: Vector<VoidThread>
    // The ambient-scheduler list link. `next` = address of the next registered
    // single-threaded group (0 = end); `registered` = whether this group is
    // currently in the `__saw_exec_head` list. Set and cleared by
    // `__register`/`__unregister`; unused by MT groups (they never register).
    private next: Int
    private registered: Bool
    // The diagnostics-list link, the twin of `next`/`registered` above.
    // Separate because every group joins this one (see `__saw_bt_head`).
    private bt_next: Int
    private bt_linked: Bool
}

// The result of resuming one frame, carried out of one borrow: whether the
// frame finished and, if not, its wake reason, which is a second call on the
// same frame. Returning the `Poll` alone would need a second `with_var_ref` to
// ask `wake_reason()`. `wake` is in the park word vocabulary (0 = ready,
// > 0 = sleep nanoseconds, negative = a park).
struct __ResumeOutcome {
    done: Bool
    wake: Int
}

// The executor's side of the hidden-allocation boundary (design 234). The
// scheduler's own bookkeeping (a task slot, a worker handle, a freed slot)
// grows underneath `group.spawn`, which is a form rather than a call: there is
// no expression a `try` could sit on and no error channel to report through,
// as with an interpolation or a coroutine frame. So a refused growth panics,
// and this is the one place that decides how it reads. It names the operation
// the caller wrote (`spawn`), not `Vector.push`, because the message is the
// only thing that tells them which allocation ran out.
//
// ENTRY POINTS:
//   `__place`
//   `__start_crew`
//   `__recycle`
func __exec_alloc_or_fault(r: Result<Void, AllocError>, what: String) unsafe {
    match r {
        case Ok(_) -> {},
        case Err(e) -> panic("{}: {}", what, e)
    }
}

extension TaskGroup {
    public init() -> TaskGroup {
        let t = Vector<Box<any Resumable>?>()
        let c = Vector<Box<any __TaskCell>?>()
        let d = Vector<Bool>()
        let r = Vector<Int>()
        let a = Vector<Bool>()
        let gn = Vector<Int>()
        let fr = Vector<Int>()
        let pn = Vector<Bool>()
        let fd = Vector<Int>()
        let fa = Vector<Int>()
        let cw = Vector<VoidThread>()
        TaskGroup(tasks: move t, cells: move c, done: move d, remaining: move r, active: move a, gen: move gn, free: move fr, pin: move pn, frame_desc: move fd, frame_addr: move fa, workers: 1, lock: None, cond: None, started: false, stopping: false, crew: move cw, next: 0, registered: false, bt_next: 0, bt_linked: false)
    }

    /// Creates a group that runs its tasks on `threads` worker threads.
    ///
    /// At runtime `threads: 1` (or less) is the same single-threaded engine as
    /// `TaskGroup()`: no OS threads and no lock. Two or more start a worker pool
    /// at the group's first spawn. Whatever the value, this spelling makes a
    /// frame spawned into the group subject to the `Send` check, so a task that
    /// borrows from its spawner needs `TaskGroup()` instead. Returns
    /// `Err(AllocError)` if the run-queue lock cannot be allocated.
    public init(threads: Int) unsafe -> Result<TaskGroup, AllocError> {
        let t = Vector<Box<any Resumable>?>()
        let c = Vector<Box<any __TaskCell>?>()
        let d = Vector<Bool>()
        let r = Vector<Int>()
        let a = Vector<Bool>()
        let gn = Vector<Int>()
        let fr = Vector<Int>()
        let pn = Vector<Bool>()
        var w = threads
        if w < 1 {
            w = 1
        }
        let fd = Vector<Int>()
        let fa = Vector<Int>()
        let cw = Vector<VoidThread>()
        var lk: UnsafePointer<Int8>? = None
        var cv: UnsafePointer<Int8>? = None
        if w >= 2 {
            // An MT group must never exist without its lock: every MT path
            // guards on the lock and does nothing without it, so no spawned
            // task would run, `Deinit` would drop every frame unrun, and
            // `Task.join` would force-unwrap a result that was never written.
            //
            // One 128-byte block holds both, the mutex at +0 and the condition
            // variable at +64 (conservative 64-byte slots). They are one
            // object, so they are one allocation and one failure.
            if let block = GlobalAllocator().alloc(128, 16) {
                __saw_rt_pthread_mutex_init_default(block)
                __saw_rt_pthread_cond_init_default(block + 64)
                lk = block
                cv = block + 64
            } else {
                // The refusal is the caller's to see. Nothing has been
                // installed yet, so an `Err` here leaves no half-built group
                // behind: the vectors above own no storage.
                return AllocError(size: 128, align: 16)
            }
        }
        return TaskGroup(tasks: move t, cells: move c, done: move d, remaining: move r, active: move a, gen: move gn, free: move fr, pin: move pn, frame_desc: move fd, frame_addr: move fa, workers: w, lock: lk, cond: cv, started: false, stopping: false, crew: move cw, next: 0, registered: false, bt_next: 0, bt_linked: false)
    }

    /// Returns the number of task slots the group holds: live tasks, plus
    /// finished tasks whose result has not been joined, plus slots pinned by
    /// `cancel_addr`. A slot is reclaimed when it has nothing left to hold;
    /// pinned slots are never reclaimed, so they keep counting after their
    /// tasks finish.
    public func count(&self) -> Int {
        self.__lock()
        let n = self.tasks.len() - self.free.len()
        self.__unlock()
        n
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The run-queue lock: one mutex over a group's scheduler state, and a no-op
    // on the single-threaded engine, which has no lock and no second thread to
    // take it against. The file header has the locking discipline and why a
    // claim moves the frame.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------

    func __lock(&self) unsafe {
        if let lk = self.lock {
            pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
        }
    }

    func __unlock(&self) unsafe {
        if let lk = self.lock {
            let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
        }
    }

    // Wake every parked worker and any thread waiting for a slot to finish.
    // Called with the lock held, after a change that could let one of them
    // proceed: a push, a recycle, or the shutdown flag. (A worker broadcasts a
    // resume's outcome itself.)
    func __notify(&self) unsafe {
        if let cv = self.cond {
            let _ = pthread_cond_broadcast(cv)
        }
    }

    // Enqueue an erased frame together with its result/cancel cell (the
    // compiler-generated `__spawn_*` calls this). Both boxes are moved in: the
    // group owns the frame until the task completes and the cell until its
    // result is joined or the group tears down. Returns the slot index (the
    // `Task` records it for slot-precise `join`). A single-threaded group
    // registers itself in the ambient scheduler list on first enqueue so the
    // shared sweep can reach its frames; an MT group runs its own worker pool
    // instead and starts it here, at the first spawn, so a group that never
    // spawns costs no threads.
    func __enqueue(&var self, task: Box<any Resumable>, cell: Box<any __TaskCell>) -> Int {
        if self.workers < 2 {
            self.__register()
        }
        // The diagnostics list takes every group, so a task dump sees MT tasks
        // too. Same address discipline as `__register`: `&self` here is the
        // group's final storage, and `Deinit` unlinks. Outside the run-queue
        // lock: the executor's workers never touch the `bt_*` fields, and
        // link/unlink (which also rewrites a neighbour's `bt_next`) take no
        // lock, on the same one-thread assumption `__unregister` states. Their
        // readers (the dump, the quiescent walk) are unsynchronized by design.
        self.__bt_link()
        self.__lock()
        let slot = self.__place(move task, move cell)
        // The broadcast is made under the lock the workers park on, so there is
        // no window between the work becoming visible and a parked worker's
        // wait ending.
        self.__notify()
        var start = false
        if self.workers >= 2 && not self.started {
            self.started = true
            start = true
        }
        self.__unlock()
        if start {
            self.__start_crew()
        }
        slot
    }

    // The slot bookkeeping itself, with the run-queue lock already held.
    func __place(&var self, task: Box<any Resumable>, cell: Box<any __TaskCell>) unsafe -> Int {
        // The dump's view of this frame, read once here: the box does not move
        // and the frame does not change type, so these two words stay true
        // until the task completes, whichever thread is running it.
        let fdesc = task.bt_desc()
        let faddr = __saw_box_data(&task) as Int
        let t: Box<any Resumable>? = move task
        let c: Box<any __TaskCell>? = move cell
        // Claim a reclaimed slot if there is one, else grow. A reused slot
        // already carries the retired generation the recycle left behind, so
        // the task landing here is distinguishable from the one that left.
        var slot = -1
        if let idx = self.free.pop() {
            slot = idx
        }
        if slot < 0 {
            slot = self.tasks.len()
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.tasks.push(None), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.cells.push(None), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.done.push(false), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.remaining.push(0), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.active.push(false), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            // generation 1: 0 names no task
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.gen.push(1), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.pin.push(false), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.frame_desc.push(-1), "TaskGroup.spawn")
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.frame_addr.push(0), "TaskGroup.spawn")
        }
        self.tasks.set(slot, move t)
        self.cells.set(slot, move c)
        self.done.set(slot, false)
        self.remaining.set(slot, 0)
        self.active.set(slot, false)
        self.frame_desc.set(slot, fdesc)
        self.frame_addr.set(slot, faddr)
        slot
    }

    // Start the pool. Runs outside the run-queue lock, on the owning thread
    // only, once per group: `started` was flipped under the lock by the enqueue
    // that got here. Each worker gets the group's address as a `Send` Int (the
    // group is `NoMove`, so that address is good for the group's whole life).
    func __start_crew(&var self) unsafe {
        let addr = (&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int
        var i = 0
        while i < self.workers {
            var t = Thread.spawn { [addr] in __saw_exec_worker(addr) }
            // An executor worker is not a user task, so it is taken back out of
            // the thread-engine live count spawn codegen just added it to. The
            // count answers "could a `Thread.spawn {}` body still run and
            // publish something"; a worker publishes nothing of its own
            // (whatever it runs is a frame in this group, which the quiescent
            // walk reads directly). Counting workers would mean an MT program
            // could never report a deadlock, the case the report is most needed
            // for. `__shutdown_crew` puts the count back ahead of the join that
            // drops it, so the pair stays balanced.
            __saw_exec_thread_task_ended()
            __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.crew.push(move t), "TaskGroup(threads:)")
            i = i + 1
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The slot-identity trio (design 134), under the run-queue lock.
    //
    // `__gen_at`, `__stale`, `__recycle` and `__pin` read and write
    // `gen`/`free`/`pin`, and on an MT group those have concurrent writers. A
    // handle cannot leave the thread that made it (`Task`/`VoidTask` hold
    // `UnsafePointer` fields, which are not `Send`), but the pool is alive
    // whenever a handle is used, and the second writer is the worker:
    // `__complete` recycles the slot of any `Void` task it finishes, which
    // advances `gen`, clears `cells` and pushes onto `free` while the owning
    // thread may be popping `free` in `__place`. A lock-free version would need
    // a slot's (index, generation) pair and its free-list membership to move
    // atomically together, a CAS-tagged free list, for no measured benefit,
    // since each of these calls already sits next to a lock acquisition.
    //
    // So the four bodies below expect the lock held. The `_sync` wrappers
    // beside them take it, and their callers are `Task.join`, `.cancel`,
    // `.cancel_addr` and the three `VoidTask` twins; `__detach_slot` takes it
    // itself. Callers that do not take it: `__complete` (a worker calls it with
    // the lock already held), `__saw_exec_should_stop`, whose group is always
    // single-threaded (the ambient sweep never walks an MT group), and the
    // synthesized spawn helper, which reads `__gen_at` after `__enqueue` has
    // unlocked. On an MT group that read races a worker that completes and
    // recycles a `Void` task first, and the handle then records the retired
    // generation (open: SL-392).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------

    // This slot's current generation: half of the (index, generation) pair
    // every handle records at spawn.
    func __gen_at(&self, slot: Int) -> Int {
        self.gen.get(slot)!
    }

    // Does `(slot, generation)` still name the task it was minted for? A handle to
    // a task whose slot has been reclaimed answers `true` here, and every handle
    // operation checks it before touching the slot or the cell it points at, so
    // a stale handle is a defined outcome, never a read of a successor's state.
    func __stale(&self, slot: Int, generation: Int) -> Bool {
        if slot < 0 || slot >= self.gen.len() {
            return true
        }
        self.gen.get(slot)! != generation
    }

    // Retire `slot`. Its generation always advances, so every handle minted
    // for the departing task goes stale at once. A slot whose raw cancel
    // address escaped through `cancel_addr()` stops there: a bare `Int` carries no
    // generation for the peer to check, so the cell has to stay valid and the
    // index is never handed out again. Every other slot releases its cell and
    // joins the free list.
    func __recycle(&var self, slot: Int) {
        self.gen.set(slot, self.gen.get(slot)! + 1)
        if self.pin.get(slot)! {
            return
        }
        self.cells.set(slot, None)
        // The free list never exceeds the slot count, so this push has been
        // reserved for by the growth that made the slot; the fault is here for
        // the same reason the growth's is.
        __exec_alloc_or_fault(self.free.push(slot), "TaskGroup slot recycle")
    }

    // `cancel_addr()` handed a peer this slot's raw cancel-word address, so the
    // slot gives up reuse for the group's lifetime (see `__recycle`).
    func __pin(&var self, slot: Int, generation: Int) {
        if self.__stale(slot, generation) {
            return
        }
        self.pin.set(slot, true)
    }

    // ---- the handle-side funnel ----------------------------------------------
    // The three above, each wrapped in the run-queue lock. A handle never
    // calls the raw forms without the lock.

    func __stale_sync(&self, slot: Int, generation: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
        self.__lock()
        let s = self.__stale(slot, generation)
        self.__unlock()
        s
    }

    func __recycle_sync(&var self, slot: Int) unsafe {
        self.__lock()
        self.__recycle(slot)
        // A recycle changes slot-identity state, so it broadcasts like every
        // other change; no current waiter depends on it.
        self.__notify()
        self.__unlock()
    }

    func __pin_sync(&var self, slot: Int, generation: Int) unsafe {
        self.__lock()
        self.__pin(slot, generation)
        self.__unlock()
    }

    // Wait until `slot` is complete, or its generation has retired, which says
    // the same thing about the task the caller asked about. The workers
    // broadcast after every resume, so this sleeps at no cost and wakes
    // whenever the answer can have changed.
    //
    // The waiter is always the group's owning thread (a handle is not `Send`,
    // so it cannot reach a worker), which is what makes waiting on the
    // run-queue condition variable safe: no worker is ever inside this.
    func __await_slot(&var self, slot: Int, generation: Int) unsafe {
        // The owner is inside the executor for the whole wait (it cannot run a
        // line of its own program until this task completes), which is what
        // lets a worker of this group raise a deadlock report.
        __saw_exec_enter()
        self.__await_slot_inner(slot, generation)
        __saw_exec_leave()
    }

    func __await_slot_inner(&var self, slot: Int, generation: Int) unsafe {
        if let cv = self.cond {
            if let lk = self.lock {
                pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
                var waiting = true
                while waiting {
                    if self.__stale(slot, generation) {
                        waiting = false
                    } else if self.done.get(slot)! {
                        waiting = false
                    } else {
                        let _ = pthread_cond_wait(cv, lk)
                    }
                }
                let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
            }
        }
    }

    // A task reached Done: mark the slot and release its frame box here, the
    // whole allocation and not just the values inside it. That is safe because
    // the result and cancel word a `Task` addresses live in the cell, which
    // stays, so the frame (params, locals held across suspends, embedded callee
    // frames, scheduler words) dies with the task that owned it (design 134).
    //
    // Every scan skips a done slot before touching `tasks`, so the `None` left
    // behind is never dereferenced. The MT caller holds the run-queue lock.
    //
    // The slot goes too when there is nothing left to hold. A task with a
    // joinable result keeps it until `join` takes the value (or until group
    // teardown drops it once); a `Void` task leaves nothing behind, so its slot
    // is reclaimed here. That keeps an accept-loop group O(live) rather than
    // O(tasks ever spawned).
    func __complete(&var self, slot: Int) {
        self.done.set(slot, true)
        self.remaining.set(slot, 0)
        self.active.set(slot, false)
        self.tasks.set(slot, None)
        // The frame is gone, so the dump's view of it goes with it.
        self.frame_desc.set(slot, -1)
        self.frame_addr.set(slot, 0)
        // A detached task's result is dropped where the task ends, not kept for
        // a `join` that was explicitly given up. That leaves the cell holding
        // nothing joinable, so the slot recycles here on a `Void` task's terms.
        let detached = self.cells.with_ref<Bool>(slot, { c in c!.__is_detached() })
        if detached {
            let _ = self.cells.with_var_ref<Bool>(slot, { c in c!.__drop_result(); true })
        }
        var keeps = false
        if not detached {
            keeps = self.cells.with_ref<Bool>(slot, { c in c!.__carries_result() })
        }
        if not keeps {
            self.__recycle(slot)
        }
    }

    // Mark this slot's cell detached and, when the task has already finished,
    // drop the result it was holding for a join. Reached only from
    // `Task.detach`/`VoidTask.detach`, which have already written the handle's
    // fate word.
    //
    // A stale (slot, generation) is a defined no-op, as it is for `cancel`: the
    // task finished and its slot retired, so there is nothing left to detach
    // and no successor this handle may reach.
    func __detach_slot(&var self, slot: Int, generation: Int) unsafe {
        self.__lock()
        if not self.__stale(slot, generation) {
            let _ = self.cells.with_var_ref<Bool>(slot, { c in c!.__detach_now(); true })
            if self.done.get(slot)! {
                let _ = self.cells.with_var_ref<Bool>(slot, { c in c!.__drop_result(); true })
                self.__recycle(slot)
            }
        }
        self.__unlock()
    }

    // The exit path of the background group: cancel every member that is still
    // live, then rouse a blocked reactor poll so one that is already io-parked
    // observes it now. The join is the caller's next line.
    func __cancel_every_live(&var self) unsafe {
        var i = 0
        while i < self.tasks.len() {
            let d = self.done.get(i)!
            if not d {
                let _ = self.cells.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { c in c!.__cancel_now(); true })
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        __saw_exec_reactor_wake()
    }

    // The slot release `Deinit` does, as a method: `__saw_bg_close` needs the
    // same teardown for a group that is not a local, whose `Deinit` never runs.
    // Clearing a slot's cell runs the erased teardown, so an unconsumed result
    // drops exactly once (a joined or detached task's cell holds `None` by then
    // and drops nothing).
    func __release_slots(&var self) unsafe {
        var s = 0
        while s < self.tasks.len() {
            self.tasks.set(s, None)
            self.cells.set(s, None)
            s = s + 1
        }
    }

    // Link this single-threaded group into the ambient scheduler list
    // (idempotent). `&self` is the group's final storage address here (called on
    // the real binding via a `&var self` method or a `__group[0]` pointer deref),
    // so the recorded address stays valid until `__unregister` in Deinit.
    func __register(&var self) unsafe {
        if self.registered {
            return
        }
        self.next = __saw_exec_head.load()
        __saw_exec_head.store((&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int)
        self.registered = true
    }

    // Link this group into the diagnostics list (idempotent). The twin of
    // `__register`, and deliberately not folded into it: that one is a
    // scheduling membership an MT group must stay out of, this one is a "there
    // are tasks here" membership every group belongs to.
    func __bt_link(&var self) unsafe {
        if self.bt_linked {
            return
        }
        self.bt_next = __saw_bt_head.load()
        __saw_bt_head.store((&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int)
        self.bt_linked = true
    }

    // Unlink from the diagnostics list (Deinit). Walks the intrusive list to fix
    // the predecessor's `bt_next`, as `__unregister` does.
    func __bt_unlink(&var self) unsafe {
        if not self.bt_linked {
            return
        }
        let me = (&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int
        let head = __saw_bt_head.load()
        if head == me {
            __saw_bt_head.store(self.bt_next)
            self.bt_linked = false
            return
        }
        var cur = head
        while cur != 0 {
            let cg = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
            let nx = cg[0].bt_next
            if nx == me {
                cg[0].bt_next = self.bt_next
                self.bt_linked = false
                return
            }
            cur = nx
        }
        self.bt_linked = false
    }

    // Unlink this group from the ambient scheduler list (Deinit, after its
    // members have drained). Walks the intrusive list to fix the predecessor's
    // `next`. Takes no lock: the ambient list is only ever linked, unlinked and
    // swept by single-threaded groups' owners, assumed to be one thread.
    func __unregister(&var self) unsafe {
        if not self.registered {
            return
        }
        let me = (&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int
        let head = __saw_exec_head.load()
        if head == me {
            __saw_exec_head.store(self.next)
            self.registered = false
            return
        }
        var cur = head
        while cur != 0 {
            let cg = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
            let nx = cg[0].next
            if nx == me {
                cg[0].next = self.next
                self.registered = false
                return
            }
            cur = nx
        }
        self.registered = false
    }

    // Retire the pool: what an MT group's `Deinit` does.
    //
    // This is a structured join, not a cancellation: `stopping` says "no more
    // work is coming", and a worker leaves only when it is set and the group has
    // no live task left, so every child still runs to completion.
    //
    // The broadcast is the whole notification; no reactor wake is owed beside
    // it. Take the worker states one at a time: a worker mid-resume re-scans as
    // soon as it finishes; one parked on the condition variable is what the
    // broadcast wakes; one in the back-off nap or a readiness-word poll is
    // bounded at 1 ms, and one in the io poll at the 50 ms cap or a live
    // task's deadline. What remains is a worker in a timer poll, whose
    // deadline also belongs to a live sleeper, so teardown has to wait for it
    // either way and cutting the poll short would buy nothing. The obvious
    // extra wake is not free: it would create the process reactor (an fd and an
    // allocation) in programs that never touch it.
    //
    // Idempotent, and cheap on a group that never spawned: no pool was started,
    // so there is nothing to tell and nothing to join.
    func __shutdown_crew(&var self) unsafe {
        if not self.started {
            return
        }
        // As in `__await_slot`: the owner is blocked in the worker joins below
        // and can run nothing of its own until they return, so a worker holding
        // a channel-parked task may report from here.
        __saw_exec_enter()
        self.__shutdown_crew_inner()
        __saw_exec_leave()
    }

    func __shutdown_crew_inner(&var self) unsafe {
        self.__lock()
        self.stopping = true
        self.__notify()
        self.__unlock()
        while self.crew.len() > 0 {
            if let ht = self.crew.pop() {
                var h = move ht
                // Restore the count `__start_crew` took this worker out of, so
                // the join below leaves it where it started.
                __saw_exec_thread_task_started()
                h.join()
            }
        }
        self.started = false
    }
}

// One slot's scheduler-visible state, read together. The worker and the ambient
// sweep scan every slot in a group asking the same three questions before
// deciding what to do with it.
struct __SlotState {
    done: Bool
    active: Bool
    remaining: Int
}

func __saw_exec_slot_state(g: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>, i: Int) unsafe -> __SlotState {
    __SlotState(done: g[0].done.get(i)!, active: g[0].active.get(i)!, remaining: g[0].remaining.get(i)!)
}

// The completion update after a resume: mark the slot Done (`__complete` retires
// it, and recycles it if nothing keeps its result) or record the wake reason it
// should resume at next. `active` is cleared first on both paths (`__complete`
// clears it again on the Done path), so both callers see the same order: clear
// `active`, then decide.
func __saw_exec_finish_resume(g: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>, idx: Int, oc: __ResumeOutcome) unsafe {
    g[0].active.set(idx, false)
    if oc.done {
        g[0].__complete(idx)
    } else {
        g[0].remaining.set(idx, oc.wake)
    }
}

// One worker thread's body. It rebuilds the group pointer from the address (an
// Int crosses the `spawn` boundary as `Send`), then loops claiming and resuming
// runnable frames for the group's whole life.
//
// The mutex serializes every scheduling touch of the group's state. A claim
// moves the frame out of its slot (`swap_out`), so the resume, which runs with
// the lock released, holds no borrow into a vector the owning thread may grow
// underneath it (see the file header). The frame goes back into its slot
// before the outcome is recorded, so a done frame is released by `__complete`
// from the one place that owns it.
//
// A worker leaves only when the group is `stopping` and nothing is live
// (structured join), and it parks on the run-queue condition variable
// whenever the group has nothing at all to run. Returns `Void`, so its
// `Thread.spawn` body needs no result slot.
func __saw_exec_worker(addr: Int) unsafe {
    let g = addr as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    if let lk = g[0].lock {
      if let cv = g[0].cond {
        var running = true
        while running {
            pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
            let n = g[0].tasks.len()
            var live = 0
            var idx = -1
            var anyactive = false
            var anyio = false
            var anyflag = false
            var minrem = -1
            // Entry point 3 of the park-deadline funnel. One clock read per
            // scan, as in the ambient sweep, so every frame in the scan is
            // judged against one instant.
            let now = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() as Int
            var i = 0
            while i < n {
                let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, i)
                if not st.done {
                    live = live + 1
                    if st.active {
                        anyactive = true
                    } else {
                        if st.remaining == 0 {
                            if idx < 0 {
                                idx = i
                            }
                        } else if __park_is_io(st.remaining) {
                            // An io park whose deadline has already arrived is
                            // promoted here, as the readiness-word arm below
                            // promotes: the scan holds the lock and the slot,
                            // and a promoted frame is just the next `idx`. One
                            // still in date folds its remaining span into the
                            // poll bound instead.
                            let dl = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Int>(i, { f in
                                f!.io_deadline()
                            })
                            if __park_deadline_expired(dl, now) {
                                g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                                if idx < 0 {
                                    idx = i
                                }
                            } else {
                                anyio = true      // io-parked
                                minrem = __park_deadline_fold(minrem, dl, now)
                            }
                        } else if __park_is_flag(st.remaining) {
                            // Parked on a readiness word: promote it here rather
                            // than in a second pass (the scan already holds the
                            // lock and the slot, and a promoted frame is just the
                            // next `idx`). A cancelled receiver is promoted too:
                            // it resumes into the check at its loop top and ends
                            // with `Err(Cancelled)` (see `__saw_exec_wake_flags`).
                            var promote = __park_flag_set(st.remaining)
                            if not promote {
                                promote = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in
                                    f!.is_cancelled()
                                })
                            }
                            if promote {
                                g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                                if idx < 0 {
                                    idx = i
                                }
                            } else {
                                anyflag = true
                            }
                        } else {
                            if minrem < 0 || st.remaining < minrem {
                                minrem = st.remaining
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                i = i + 1
            }
            if live == 0 {
                let stopping = g[0].stopping
                if stopping {
                    // Told to stop, and nothing left to finish: this is the only
                    // way out of the loop, and it is what makes `Deinit` a
                    // structured join rather than a cancellation.
                    let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                    running = false
                } else {
                    // Idle: park until the owning thread spawns something or
                    // tears the group down. `pthread_cond_wait` releases the
                    // lock atomically, so there is no window between deciding to
                    // wait and waiting; every state change that could end the
                    // wait broadcasts under this same lock.
                    let _ = pthread_cond_wait(cv, lk)
                    let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                }
            } else {
                if idx >= 0 {
                    // Claim frame `idx`: mark the slot active and take the frame
                    // box out of it, so the resume below owns the box outright
                    // and the run queue is free to grow while it runs.
                    g[0].active.set(idx, true)
                    var frame = g[0].tasks.swap_out(idx, None)
                    let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                    let oc = match frame!.resume() {
                        case Pending -> __ResumeOutcome(done: false, wake: frame!.wake_reason()),
                        case Done -> __ResumeOutcome(done: true, wake: 0)
                    }
                    pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
                    // Back into its slot before the outcome is recorded, so the
                    // Done path's `__complete` releases the box from the one
                    // place that owns it.
                    g[0].tasks.set(idx, move frame)
                    __saw_exec_finish_resume(g, idx, oc)
                    let _ = pthread_cond_broadcast(cv)
                    let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                    if oc.done {
                        // A completion is what a joining thread and any parked
                        // ambient scheduler are waiting for; the condvar covers
                        // the first and the reactor wake the second.
                        __saw_exec_reactor_wake()
                    }
                } else {
                    if anyactive {
                        // Nothing runnable, but a peer is mid-resume and may make
                        // a task ready. Back off briefly and re-scan. Not the
                        // condition variable: the peer's broadcast would serve,
                        // but a bounded nap also covers the changes no broadcast
                        // on this group reports (a readiness word or an io latch
                        // written by another thread, a sleeper's deadline
                        // passing), and here a fixed millisecond is cheaper than
                        // getting the wake matrix exactly right.
                        let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                        __saw_exec_sleep_ns(BACKOFF_NANOS)
                    } else if anyio {
                        // An idle worker polls the reactor with a bounded
                        // timeout: the earliest sleep deadline, else a 50 ms
                        // cap. Bounded because each one-shot event is delivered
                        // to one poller, possibly another worker or another
                        // thread's scheduler, so a worker whose event went
                        // elsewhere must come back and re-read the latch rather
                        // than block. Poll outside the lock, then promote only
                        // the io-parked tasks whose wake word the reactor
                        // latched (`wake_reason()` >= 0), not the herd. The
                        // latch is a persistent word, so a latch another
                        // thread's poll set is seen at the next scan (one the
                        // frame itself overwrites while parking is lost:
                        // SL-353).
                        // Advance sleepers only if the poll timed out
                        // (events == 0). The scan runs under the lock, so the
                        // `with_var_ref` borrow never races a peer worker.
                        //
                        // The queue length is re-read after the lock is
                        // retaken: the owning thread may have spawned into this
                        // group while the poll was blocked, so the pre-poll `n`
                        // is only a lower bound.
                        var tmo = minrem
                        if tmo < 0 {
                            tmo = MT_POLL_CAP_NANOS
                        }
                        // A readiness-word park beside the io one is woken by a
                        // write this poll is not watching, so shorten the cap to
                        // the re-scan bound while one is live.
                        tmo = __saw_exec_flag_bound(tmo, anyflag)
                        let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                        let events = __saw_host_reactor().poll(__saw_exec_poll_ms(tmo))
                        pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
                        // Read the clock after the poll: the poll is where a
                        // deadline's time actually goes by.
                        let post = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() as Int
                        let kn = g[0].tasks.len()
                        var k = 0
                        while k < kn {
                            let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, k)
                            if not st.done {
                                if __park_is_io(st.remaining) {
                                    // Promote a latched, a cancelled (it re-checks
                                    // `cancelled()` and bails) or a timed-out io
                                    // park; the rest stay parked.
                                    let wk = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(k, { f in
                                        __park_io_runnable(f!.wake_reason(), f!.is_cancelled(), f!.io_deadline(), post)
                                    })
                                    if wk {
                                        g[0].remaining.set(k, 0)
                                    }
                                } else if st.remaining > 0 && events == 0 && tmo > 0 {
                                    // Charge what the poll waited (`tmo`), not
                                    // the earliest deadline: the flag bound may
                                    // have cut it short, and crediting the full
                                    // span would wake every sleeper early.
                                    var nr = st.remaining - tmo
                                    if nr < 0 {
                                        nr = 0
                                    }
                                    g[0].remaining.set(k, nr)
                                }
                            }
                            k = k + 1
                        }
                        let _ = pthread_cond_broadcast(cv)
                        let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                        if events > 0 {
                            // This poll consumed events out of a reactor several
                            // threads share, and the tokens it latched may belong
                            // to frames another thread schedules: the ambient
                            // scheduler may be blocked in an unbounded poll on
                            // the event this one took. The wake sends it back
                            // round to read its own latch. `events` counts a
                            // consumed wake event too, so a worker this wake
                            // rouses in this branch wakes the reactor again;
                            // SL-353 records the resulting feedback loop.
                            __saw_exec_reactor_wake()
                        }
                    } else if anyflag && minrem < 0 {
                        // Every live task of this group is parked on a
                        // readiness word and none is sleeping. Nothing this
                        // worker can do makes one ready (the writer is another
                        // thread), so park in the reactor, where a
                        // `__saw_exec_chan_wake` poke ends the wait at once,
                        // bounded, so a poke that races the poll costs a re-scan
                        // rather than the wake. The scan at the loop top reads
                        // the word when this returns. (Not the run-queue
                        // condition variable: its broadcasts all happen under
                        // this group's lock, and a readiness word is written by
                        // a thread that holds no group lock at all, so a wait on
                        // it could miss the write outright.)
                        let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                        // With the lock released: the walk reads every group,
                        // this one included, takes no lock of its own, and naps
                        // before it confirms.
                        __saw_exec_check_quiescent(false)
                        let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(
                            __saw_exec_poll_ms(FLAG_POLL_NANOS))
                    } else {
                        // Every live task is sleeping (or parked on a readiness
                        // word beside a sleeper) and none is in flight: park for
                        // the earliest deadline, then charge every sleeper the
                        // time that passed, capped at that deadline, so the
                        // shortest wakes first. The park is a reactor poll, not
                        // a plain sleep, so a peer's `cancel()` wake ends the
                        // nap instead of waiting it out. The poll runs outside
                        // the lock (it can block for the whole deadline, and a
                        // worker holding the queue lock that long would wedge
                        // the pool); the scan that follows retakes it, as the
                        // io branch does.
                        var spent = minrem
                        if minrem > 0 {
                            let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                            let before = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos()
                            // Shortened while a readiness-word park is live. The
                            // elapsed measurement below charges sleepers what
                            // actually passed, so a cut-short poll needs nothing
                            // else said about it.
                            let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(
                                __saw_exec_poll_ms(
                                    __saw_exec_flag_bound(minrem, anyflag)))
                            let took = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() - before
                            if took > (0 as Int64) && took < (minrem as Int64) {
                                spent = took as Int
                            }
                            pthread_mutex_lock(lk)
                        }
                        let jn = g[0].tasks.len()   // re-read: see the io branch
                        var j = 0
                        while j < jn {
                            let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, j)
                            if not st.done {
                                if st.remaining > 0 {
                                    // A cancelled sleeper becomes runnable now
                                    // rather than at its deadline, as a
                                    // cancelled io park does.
                                    let ck = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(j, { f in
                                        f!.is_cancelled()
                                    })
                                    if ck {
                                        g[0].remaining.set(j, 0)
                                    } else {
                                        var nr = st.remaining - spent
                                        if nr < 0 {
                                            nr = 0
                                        }
                                        g[0].remaining.set(j, nr)
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                            j = j + 1
                        }
                        let _ = pthread_cond_broadcast(cv)
                        let _ = pthread_mutex_unlock(lk)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
      }
    }
}

// Deinit joins every child (structured join at scope exit), then releases
// whatever the slots still hold: an unfinished frame's box, and every cell,
// dropping any unconsumed result exactly once (a joined task's result was
// already taken, leaving `None` behind).
extension TaskGroup: NoCopy {
    public func deinit(&var self) unsafe {
        if self.workers >= 2 {
            // MT: tell the pool no more work is coming and join it. The workers
            // finish every live task first, so this is a structured join, not
            // a cancellation.
            self.__shutdown_crew()
        } else {
            // ST structured join: drive the ambient scheduler until every frame
            // of this group is done (siblings in other groups run meanwhile),
            // then unlink from the ambient list. Frames in other groups are
            // untouched; this drops only its own boxes.
            let me = (&self) as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> as Int
            __saw_exec_run(me, -1, 0)
            self.__unregister()
        }
        // Leave the diagnostics list before this group's storage goes away,
        // whichever engine ran it.
        self.__bt_unlink()
        // Release every slot explicitly, here rather than in the memberwise
        // field cleanup, so an unconsumed result drops while the group is still
        // whole (and before the run-queue mutex goes away). Clearing a slot's
        // cell runs the erased teardown (the vtable destructor runs the cell's
        // memberwise cleanup), which drops an unconsumed result exactly once; a
        // joined task's cell holds `None` by then and drops nothing. `tasks` is
        // already `None` for every completed task (the frame box is released at
        // completion); only a frame the join could not finish is left.
        self.__release_slots()
        // Release the run-queue mutex and its condition variable (multi-threaded
        // groups only): one 128-byte block, so one deallocation. Safe here
        // because `__shutdown_crew` above joined every worker, so nothing is
        // waiting on either.
        if let lk = self.lock {
            if let cv = self.cond {
                let _ = pthread_cond_destroy(cv)
            }
            pthread_mutex_destroy(lk)
            GlobalAllocator().dealloc(lk, 128, 16)
        }
    }
}

// A group is a scope (design 124): its Deinit joins its children where it was
// born, and handles and workers reach the run queue through its address, so
// it cannot be relocated. `NoMove` requires the NoCopy above rather than
// implying it: duplication and relocation are separate properties, and a
// group states both.
extension TaskGroup: NoMove {}

// The typed handle for one spawned task. It owns nothing: raw pointers into
// the group and into the task's group-owned cell. `result_ptr` addresses the
// cell's `T?` slot and `cancel_ptr` its cancel word; the cell is a stable heap
// allocation that outlives the frame, so neither pointer is disturbed when the
// task completes and its frame box is released. Dropping an unjoined
// `group.spawn` handle is fine: the result stays in the cell and is dropped
// once at group teardown.
/// A handle to a spawned task that returns a value.
///
/// `join` waits for the task and takes its result. A handle from `group.spawn`
/// may be dropped unjoined, and the result is released when the group is torn
/// down. A handle from `Task.spawn` must be joined, detached or cancelled
/// before it is dropped.
public struct Task<T> {
    private result_ptr: UnsafePointer<T?>
    private cancel_ptr: UnsafePointer<Bool>
    // The cell's `__fated` word. `join`/`detach`/`cancel` write it and `deinit`
    // reads it through the cell, because the cell is what outlives the task and
    // a handle's destructor may run long after its frame is gone.
    private fate_ptr: UnsafePointer<Bool>
    // Provenance, not type (design 242). A handle minted by `Task.spawn`
    // carries the must-consume obligation and faults when it is dropped
    // unconsumed; one minted by `group.spawn` is free to drop, because the
    // group is a declared consumer. The two are one type, so the bit tells them
    // apart; keying the fault on the type would break the accept-loop idiom.
    private background: Bool
    private group_ptr: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    // This task's slot index in its group's queue, paired with the generation
    // the slot carried when the task was spawned. `join` returns as soon as its
    // own task finishes (siblings may still run); the generation is what tells
    // a live task apart from a successor occupying the same reclaimed index.
    private slot: Int
    private generation: Int
}

extension Task<T> {
    // Write the fate word, and only for a background handle. A `group.spawn`
    // handle's cell is released the moment its slot recycles (a `Void` task
    // recycles at completion), so its `fate_ptr` may address freed storage by
    // the time a consume runs; nothing reads that word for a group handle,
    // since one may drop freely. A background slot is pinned at birth, so its
    // cell outlives every handle to it and the word is always there to write.
    func __write_fate(&self) unsafe {
        if self.background {
            self.fate_ptr[0] = true
        }
    }

    // Wait for this task (driving the ambient scheduler on a single-threaded
    // group), then take its result. `take()` moves the payload out of the cell
    // and leaves `None`, so the cell's later teardown drops nothing. Taking the
    // result retires the slot's generation (and frees the slot unless it is
    // pinned), so this handle and any copy of the task's identity go stale.
    //
    // Joining twice therefore panics rather than returning a second value:
    // there is only one result, and this call already moved it out.
    /// Waits for the task to complete and returns its result, moving it out of
    /// the group. Calling `join` a second time panics.
    public func join(&self) unsafe -> T {
        // The fate is written here, before anything can fail, so a handle that
        // reached a consume never faults in `deinit`.
        self.__write_fate()
        if self.group_ptr[0].__stale_sync(self.slot, self.generation) {
            panic("Task.join: this task's result was already joined")
        }
        if self.group_ptr[0].workers >= 2 {
            // MT: the pool is already running this task, so a join waits for it
            // rather than driving it, at no cost, on the run-queue condition
            // variable a worker broadcasts after every resume. The workers stay
            // alive afterwards, so a second join into the same group starts
            // nothing.
            self.group_ptr[0].__await_slot(self.slot, self.generation)
        } else {
            // Drive the ambient scheduler until this frame's slot is done.
            // Reentrancy-safe: if the joining task is itself running on the
            // scheduler (task joins task), the sweep skips frames active on the
            // C stack and never re-enters a live coroutine.
            __saw_exec_run(self.group_ptr as Int, self.slot, self.generation)
        }
        let r = self.result_ptr[0].take()!
        self.group_ptr[0].__recycle_sync(self.slot)
        move r
    }

    // Cooperative cancellation, with no forced destroy: set the cell's
    // `__cancel` word. The task observes it where it checks (`cancelled()`) or
    // at a cancellation-aware wait, and returns through normal control flow.
    // Cancelling a task that has already finished and been reclaimed is a
    // defined no-op: there is nothing left to ask to stop, and the stale handle
    // must not reach a successor sharing its slot.
    /// Asks the task to stop.
    ///
    /// Cancellation is cooperative: the task observes it through `cancelled()`
    /// or at a cancellation-aware wait, and returns through its own control
    /// flow. Cancelling a task that has already finished is a no-op.
    public func cancel(&self) unsafe {
        // Cancelling is a fate: the task still runs its cancel path to
        // completion, which is a decision about how it ends.
        self.__write_fate()
        if self.group_ptr[0].__stale_sync(self.slot, self.generation) {
            return
        }
        self.cancel_ptr[0] = true
        // Rouse a blocked reactor poll so a target that is already io-parked
        // observes the cancel now, not only at its next check.
        __saw_exec_reactor_wake()
    }

    // Hand the task to the process, on the scheduler side only: there is no
    // thread to hand to an OS and no runtime seam. The task's cell is marked
    // detached, so its result is dropped where the task ends instead of being
    // kept for a `join` that will never come, and the handle's fate is written.
    // The task itself is untouched and keeps running.
    //
    // The declared drop is what satisfies never-hide-errors: `detach()` is the
    // moral `let _ =`, said out loud at the one place that knows the value is
    // being thrown away.
    /// Hands the task to the process and gives up its result.
    ///
    /// The task keeps running; its result is dropped when it completes, and this
    /// handle's obligation is discharged. A task still live when the program's
    /// background group closes is cancelled there, like any other member.
    public func detach(&self) unsafe {
        if not self.background {
            // A group handle takes no `detach()`. A group task may borrow from
            // its spawner and the group's scope is what releases that borrow,
            // so "hand it to the process" has no meaning for one. Provenance
            // decides, not the type, and a caller-checkable bug is a fault.
            panic("Task.detach: this handle came from `group.spawn`, and a group task's fate is its group's scope — drop the handle instead, or use `Task.spawn` for a detachable task")
        }
        self.__write_fate()
        self.group_ptr[0].__detach_slot(self.slot, self.generation)
    }

    // The cancel word's address as a `Send` Int. A `Task` is NoCopy and holds
    // raw pointers (not `Send`), so it cannot be moved into another task, but
    // its cancel-word address can. A canceller receives this Int and sets the
    // flag (`(addr as UnsafePointer<Bool>)[0] = true`), possibly from another
    // thread; the target observes it via `cancelled()` at its next check. The
    // byte is only ever set, never cleared, so a reader that sees the write
    // late only observes the cancel later. No reactor wake goes with this
    // write, unlike `cancel()`. The address is into the task's cell, which the
    // group keeps alive for its own lifetime, so a write that lands after the
    // task finished is defined and inert. Taking the address pins the slot: it
    // keeps its cell and gives up reuse, because a bare `Int` carries no
    // generation for the peer to check against.
    /// Returns the address of this task's cancel flag as a `Send` integer, which
    /// is how a peer task cancels this one: a `Task` itself cannot cross a
    /// spawn, but the address can. The receiver cancels by writing through it,
    /// `(addr as UnsafePointer<Bool>)[0] = true`. Taking the address from a
    /// live handle pins the task's slot and gives up its reuse; while the group
    /// exists, a write that lands after that task has finished is defined and
    /// does nothing. Calling this on a handle whose task was already reaped
    /// (after `join`) does not pin anything, and the address is then not
    /// covered by that promise (SL-392).
    ///
    /// The write sets the flag and wakes nothing: the task sees it at its next
    /// `cancelled()` check, and a task parked in an io wait that nothing else
    /// wakes keeps waiting until its event or deadline arrives (SL-392). Use
    /// `cancel()` when the handle is at hand.
    public func cancel_addr(&self) unsafe -> Int {
        self.group_ptr[0].__pin_sync(self.slot, self.generation)
        self.cancel_ptr as Int
    }
}

// Owns nothing (the frame and result live in the group), so dropping an
// unjoined handle leaves the group-owned result alone.
//
// A handle minted by `Task.spawn` panics here when no fate was written
// (design 242). The check is gated on provenance, not the type, which keeps
// group handles freely droppable: the accept-loop idiom drops one per
// iteration on purpose. A compile-time check makes this path unreachable from
// ordinary code; what is left is an owner whose hand-written `deinit` was
// trusted to consume the handle and did not. A caller-checkable bug is a
// fault, not a status, and panics do not unwind, so a destructor panic cannot
// meet another on the way out.
extension Task<T>: NoCopy {
    /// Panics if a `Task.spawn` handle was neither joined, detached nor
    /// cancelled: a background task's fate is explicit, and dropping the handle
    /// is not one of the fates. A `group.spawn` handle drops silently.
    public func deinit(&var self) unsafe {
        if self.background {
            if not self.fate_ptr[0] {
                panic("Task was dropped without being joined, detached or cancelled — `join()` waits for it and takes its result, `detach()` hands it to the process, `cancel()` asks it to stop")
            }
        }
    }
}

// The handle for a spawned task whose body returns `Void`. A `Void` task
// carries no result slot (a `{..., void}` struct field is illegal LLVM), so its
// cell is a `__VoidCell`: there is no `result_ptr` and no value to take, and
// `join` only waits for the task. Cancellation and drop-if-unjoined behave as
// they do for `Task<T>`. A distinct type (not `Task<Void>`), so the generic
// handle never has to encode a zero-size `T?` result pointer.
/// A handle to a spawned task that returns `Void`.
///
/// `join` waits for the task to complete. Cancellation and dropping behave as
/// they do for `Task<T>`.
public struct VoidTask {
    private cancel_ptr: UnsafePointer<Bool>
    // See `Task<T>`'s two fields for what these are and why the fate word lives
    // in the cell rather than in the handle.
    private fate_ptr: UnsafePointer<Bool>
    private background: Bool
    private group_ptr: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    private slot: Int
    private generation: Int
}

extension VoidTask {
    // See `Task.__write_fate`: provenance-gated for the same reason.
    func __write_fate(&self) unsafe {
        if self.background {
            self.fate_ptr[0] = true
        }
    }

    // Wait until this task is done. No result to take: the void body ran for
    // its effects. A `Void` task's slot is retired at completion (nothing
    // outlives it), so joining one that already finished is a defined no-op
    // rather than a panic: the question "is it done yet" has an answer, and the
    // answer is yes.
    /// Waits until the task is done. Joining one that has already finished
    /// returns immediately.
    public func join(&self) unsafe {
        self.__write_fate()
        if self.group_ptr[0].__stale_sync(self.slot, self.generation) {
            return
        }
        if self.group_ptr[0].workers >= 2 {
            self.group_ptr[0].__await_slot(self.slot, self.generation)
        } else {
            __saw_exec_run(self.group_ptr as Int, self.slot, self.generation)
        }
    }

    /// Asks the task to stop, cooperatively, as `Task.cancel` does. Cancelling
    /// a task that has already finished is a no-op.
    public func cancel(&self) unsafe {
        self.__write_fate()
        if self.group_ptr[0].__stale_sync(self.slot, self.generation) {
            return
        }
        self.cancel_ptr[0] = true
        // Rouse a blocked reactor poll (see `Task.cancel`).
        __saw_exec_reactor_wake()
    }

    /// Hands the task to the process. The task keeps running and this handle's
    /// obligation is discharged; see `Task.detach` for the whole story. There is
    /// no result to give up here.
    public func detach(&self) unsafe {
        if not self.background {
            panic("VoidTask.detach: this handle came from `group.spawn`, and a group task's fate is its group's scope — drop the handle instead, or use `Task.spawn` for a detachable task")
        }
        self.__write_fate()
        self.group_ptr[0].__detach_slot(self.slot, self.generation)
    }

    /// Returns the cancel-flag address and pins the slot, exactly as
    /// `Task.cancel_addr` does, with the same limits after a reap and on
    /// waking.
    public func cancel_addr(&self) unsafe -> Int {
        self.group_ptr[0].__pin_sync(self.slot, self.generation)
        self.cancel_ptr as Int
    }
}

extension VoidTask: NoCopy {
    /// Panics if a `Task.spawn` handle was neither joined, detached nor
    /// cancelled, as `Task.deinit` does. A `group.spawn` handle drops silently.
    public func deinit(&var self) unsafe {
        if self.background {
            if not self.fate_ptr[0] {
                panic("VoidTask was dropped without being joined, detached or cancelled — `join()` waits for it, `detach()` hands it to the process, `cancel()` asks it to stop")
            }
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// The ambient cooperative scheduler (design 89)
// =============================================================================
// One sweep over the list of every live single-threaded group, shared by every
// drive point: round-robin over ready frames, a reactor park when none is
// ready, and frames active on the C stack skipped (reentrancy).

// Any not-done, not-active, ready (remaining == 0) frame anywhere in the list?
func __saw_exec_any_ready() unsafe -> Bool {
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            let rem_i = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) && rem_i == 0 {
                return true
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    false
}

// Does the group at `gaddr` still have a not-done frame?
func __saw_exec_group_has_live(gaddr: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    let g = gaddr as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    let n = g[0].tasks.len()
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
        if not done_i {
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    false
}

// Termination predicate. term_group == 0 -> all (no live frame anywhere);
// term_group != 0 & term_slot < 0 -> group (no live frame in that group);
// term_group != 0 & term_slot >= 0 -> frame (that group's slot is done, at the
// generation `term_gen` the joiner asked about: a reclaimed slot may already
// host a different task, and a mismatch means ours finished long ago).
func __saw_exec_should_stop(term_group: Int, term_slot: Int, term_gen: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    if term_group != 0 {
        let g = term_group as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        if term_slot >= 0 {
            if g[0].__stale(term_slot, term_gen) {
                return true
            }
            let d = g[0].done.get(term_slot)!
            return d
        }
        return not __saw_exec_group_has_live(term_group)
    }
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        if __saw_exec_group_has_live(cur) {
            return false
        }
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    true
}

// Precise reactor wakeup (design 91). The poll latched the `__wake` word of the
// frames whose (fd, direction) fired, from -1 to 0; an io-parked frame
// (remaining == -1) is made runnable only when its `wake_reason()` has become
// >= 0, so a frame still parked stays parked (no thundering herd). If the poll
// timed out (events == 0), advance sleepers by `deadline`, the span the poll
// waited. Skips frames active on the C stack.
//
// Entry point 2 of the park-deadline funnel: an io-parked frame whose own
// deadline has arrived is also made runnable. `now` is read once by the caller
// and passed in, so every frame in one sweep is judged against one instant; a
// clock read per slot could time one frame out and not its identical twin.
func __saw_exec_wake_io(events: Int, deadline: Int, now: Int) unsafe {
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) {
                let r = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
                if __park_is_io(r) {
                    // Promote an io-parked frame the reactor latched (wake word
                    // >= 0), one a peer cancelled (it re-checks `cancelled()` at
                    // its park-loop top and bails), or one whose own deadline has
                    // arrived (it re-issues its syscall and reports the timeout
                    // itself). The rest stay parked (no herd wake).
                    let woke = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in
                        __park_io_runnable(f!.wake_reason(), f!.is_cancelled(), f!.io_deadline(), now)
                    })
                    if woke {
                        g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                    }
                } else if r > 0 && events == 0 && deadline > 0 {
                    var nr = r - deadline
                    if nr < 0 {
                        nr = 0
                    }
                    g[0].remaining.set(i, nr)
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
}

// Is any io-parked frame cancelled? A peer that cancels through `cancel_addr`
// writes the task's cancel word directly, with no reactor wake. A write made
// before this pre-poll scan is caught here, so the sweep wakes the frame (it
// re-checks `cancelled()` at its park-loop top and bails) instead of blocking
// in the poll on an idle fd; a write from another thread while the sweep is
// already blocked in an unbounded poll is not seen until the poll returns.
// (`cancel()` also wakes the reactor, so a poll already blocked returns
// promptly.)
func __saw_exec_any_cancelled_io() unsafe -> Bool {
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) {
                let r = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
                if __park_is_io(r) {
                    let c = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in f!.is_cancelled() })
                    if c {
                        return true
                    }
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    false
}

// Lost-wakeup guard: is any io-parked frame's `__wake` word already latched
// (>= 0)? A one-shot event can be consumed by a poll that is not this sweep:
// an outside-frame blocking `io_wait` (a sync connect wait) polls the same
// reactor inline and latches whatever fired, including a parked sibling's
// event. The event does not fire again (a one-shot registration is removed or
// disarmed on delivery), so the sweep's own unbounded poll could block forever
// and `__saw_exec_wake_io`, the sweep's reader of the latch, would never run.
// This pre-poll scan catches a latch set before it runs; one set by another
// thread after it, while the sweep blocks, needs a reactor wake. An MT
// worker's io branch sends one after consuming events; its timer and
// readiness-word polls, and a single-frame drive polling on another thread, do
// not. An MT worker re-reads latches after each bounded poll instead.
func __saw_exec_any_latched_io() unsafe -> Bool {
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) {
                let r = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
                if __park_is_io(r) {
                    let latched = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in f!.wake_reason() >= 0 })
                    if latched {
                        return true
                    }
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    false
}

// Make every cancelled sleeper runnable, and report whether any was, so a
// cancel reaches a sleeping task now rather than at its deadline, as it
// reaches an io park. A sleeper woken here resumes right after its `sleep`,
// sees `cancelled()` at its next check and takes its cancel path.
//
// The return value is what lets the sweep skip the park entirely when a cancel
// landed while it was assembling the deadline.
func __saw_exec_wake_cancelled_sleepers() unsafe -> Bool {
    var woke = false
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) {
                let r = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
                if r > 0 {
                    let c = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in f!.is_cancelled() })
                    if c {
                        g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                        woke = true
                    }
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    woke
}

// The readiness-word counterpart of `__saw_exec_wake_io` (design 230). Promote
// every frame parked on a word that has become nonzero; one whose word is
// still clear stays parked, which is what makes a send wake the receivers of
// its channel and nobody else's. Returns whether anything was promoted.
//
// A cancelled waiter is promoted too, as `__saw_exec_wake_io` promotes a
// cancelled io park. That depends on `Channel.receive` checking `cancelled()`
// at its loop top and returning `Err(Cancelled)`: without the check, waking a
// cancelled waiter would resume it into a `try_receive` that finds nothing
// and parks again, a promote/resume/re-park spin. With it, the wake ends the
// receive, and without the wake the check would never be reached.
func __saw_exec_wake_flags() unsafe -> Bool {
    var woke = false
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, i)
            if (not st.done) && (not st.active) && __park_is_flag(st.remaining) {
                if __park_flag_set(st.remaining) {
                    g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                    woke = true
                } else {
                    let c = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in
                        f!.is_cancelled()
                    })
                    if c {
                        g[0].remaining.set(i, 0)
                        woke = true
                    }
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
    woke
}

// =============================================================================
// The quiescent deadlock report (design 230)
// =============================================================================
// The one place the report is decided and raised. ENTRY POINTS, each reached
// only when its caller has nothing left to do and a readiness-word park is the
// reason:
//   * `__saw_exec_run_inner`, phase 2's flag branch (the ambient scheduler);
//   * `__saw_exec_worker`'s flag branch (an MT worker, with its group lock
//     released);
//   * `__saw_exec_park_flag_loop` (the single-frame drive, whose own parked
//     frame is on no run queue and so is passed in as `self_parked`).
//
// The per-channel question ("will a sender ever exist for this channel") is
// not decidable: it needs to know what code will run. The quiescent state
// avoids it: publishing a readiness word requires running code, so the report
// needs only that no code in this process can run. Each of these ends the
// walk:
//   * a thread-engine body that is still counted (neither joined nor
//     detached);
//   * an owner thread outside the executor (it may be computing toward a
//     send);
//   * a frame that is ready, or active on some thread's stack;
//   * a frame parked on io (the reactor holds a registration that may fire);
//   * a frame parked on a timer (its deadline will arrive);
//   * a frame parked on a readiness word that is already set, or whose task
//     has been cancelled (it resumes into the receive loop's cancellation
//     check).
// With none of those and at least one frame parked on a clear readiness word,
// the wait can only end when the process does. Two things the walk does not
// cover: a detached thread-engine body, which is no longer counted, and a
// group past the walk's BT_MAX_GROUPS bound, which is not examined.
//
// The walk is the same unsynchronized snapshot `__saw_bt_dump` takes, over the
// diagnostics list, which carries MT groups too. Unsynchronized is fine for a
// report and not for an abort, so the state is confirmed twice with a nap
// between: when it is real, nothing can change it and the second look costs a
// millisecond; a false report would need the same inconsistent reading twice,
// a millisecond apart.
func __saw_exec_quiescent_now(self_parked: Bool) unsafe -> Bool {
    if __saw_exec_thread_live.load() != 0 {
        return false
    }
    // The owner thread must be inside the executor. Without this an MT worker
    // could report while the owner was computing its way toward the send that
    // would have ended the wait (see `__saw_exec_in_executor`).
    if __saw_exec_in_executor.load() == 0 {
        return false
    }
    var parked = self_parked
    var cur = __saw_bt_head.load()
    var seen = 0
    while cur != 0 && seen < BT_MAX_GROUPS {
        seen = seen + 1
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, i)
            if not st.done {
                if st.active {
                    return false
                }
                if __park_is_flag(st.remaining) {
                    if __park_flag_set(st.remaining) {
                        return false
                    }
                    // A cancelled waiter is resumable: the promote (in the
                    // ambient sweep or a worker's scan) takes it and its
                    // receive ends with `Err(Cancelled)`. Reading it here as
                    // well keeps a cancel that lands during the double-check
                    // from being reported as a deadlock.
                    let c = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Bool>(i, { f in
                        f!.is_cancelled()
                    })
                    if c {
                        return false
                    }
                    parked = true
                } else {
                    // Ready (0), io (-1) or a timer (> 0): something can run.
                    return false
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].bt_next
    }
    parked
}

// Set once, by whichever engine raises the report first. Two engines can reach
// the state together and the process is ending either way; one report is the
// readable one.
static __saw_exec_reported: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

func __saw_exec_check_quiescent(self_parked: Bool) unsafe {
    if not __saw_exec_quiescent_now(self_parked) {
        return
    }
    __saw_exec_sleep_ns(BACKOFF_NANOS)
    if not __saw_exec_quiescent_now(self_parked) {
        return
    }
    if not __saw_exec_reported.compare_exchange(0, 1) {
        return
    }
    // The panic path prints the task dump after this line, so the reader gets
    // which task is parked on what without asking for it.
    panic("deadlock: every task is waiting to receive from a channel, and nothing in this program can send. A channel has no sender count — every handle is the same handle — so a producer that is finished must say so with close(), and a receive on a closed, drained channel returns Err(Closed) instead of waiting")
}

// Pure-sleep advance: subtract the parked deadline from every sleeper so the
// shortest wakes first. Skips frames active on the C stack.
func __saw_exec_advance_sleep(deadline: Int) unsafe {
    var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
    while cur != 0 {
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            let done_i = g[0].done.get(i)!
            let active_i = g[0].active.get(i)!
            if (not done_i) && (not active_i) {
                let r = g[0].remaining.get(i)!
                if r > 0 {
                    var nr = r - deadline
                    if nr < 0 {
                        nr = 0
                    }
                    g[0].remaining.set(i, nr)
                }
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].next
    }
}

// The scheduler sweep. Drives ready frames round-robin across every registered
// single-threaded group until `__saw_exec_should_stop`, honouring sleep, io
// and readiness-word parks. Reentrancy-safe: a frame currently `active` on the
// C stack is skipped, so a nested drive (a task joins a task; a nested group's
// Deinit) sweeps the same list without re-entering a live coroutine, and
// returns to the outer sweep when every live frame is active further up.
func __saw_exec_run(term_group: Int, term_slot: Int, term_gen: Int) unsafe {
    // The sweeping thread is the owner thread, and while it is in here it runs
    // no program code of its own, which the frame walk cannot recover for
    // itself. Nests (a task joins a task), which is why the counter is a depth.
    __saw_exec_enter()
    __saw_exec_run_inner(term_group, term_slot, term_gen)
    __saw_exec_leave()
}

func __saw_exec_run_inner(term_group: Int, term_slot: Int, term_gen: Int) unsafe {
    while not __saw_exec_should_stop(term_group, term_slot, term_gen) {
        // Phase 1: resume every ready frame across all groups until none is ready
        // (re-checked from the head each pass, so a just-spawned frame is caught).
        //
        // Each pass promotes readiness-word parks first. A cooperative sender
        // publishes the readiness word inside its own resume, so this is the
        // step that turns a same-thread send into the receiver's next turn, and
        // it runs per pass rather than per sweep, or a value sent by the last
        // ready frame would wait for phase 2 to give up first. The promote is
        // the left operand of `||`, so it always runs.
        while __saw_exec_wake_flags() || __saw_exec_any_ready() {
            var cur = __saw_exec_head.load()
            while cur != 0 {
                let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
                let n = g[0].tasks.len()
                var i = 0
                while i < n {
                    let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, i)
                    if (not st.done) && (not st.active) && st.remaining == 0 {
                        g[0].active.set(i, true)
                        let oc = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<__ResumeOutcome>(i, { f in
                            match f!.resume() {
                                case Pending -> __ResumeOutcome(done: false, wake: f!.wake_reason()),
                                case Done -> __ResumeOutcome(done: true, wake: 0)
                            }
                        })
                        __saw_exec_finish_resume(g, i, oc)
                        if __saw_exec_should_stop(term_group, term_slot, term_gen) {
                            return
                        }
                    }
                    i = i + 1
                }
                let nx = g[0].next
                cur = nx
            }
        }
        if __saw_exec_should_stop(term_group, term_slot, term_gen) {
            return
        }
        // Phase 2: nothing ready. Scan all groups for the earliest sleep deadline,
        // whether any frame is io-parked, and whether any is parked on a
        // readiness word, then park (reactor/timer/flag).
        var deadline = -1
        var io = false
        var flag = false
        var anylive = false
        // Entry point 1 of the park-deadline funnel. One clock read for the
        // whole scan, so every frame is judged against one instant, and an io
        // park's deadline folds into the same earliest-deadline variable a
        // sleeper's does, which is what bounds the poll below with no new
        // arithmetic downstream.
        let now = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() as Int
        var cur2 = __saw_exec_head.load()
        while cur2 != 0 {
            let g = cur2 as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
            let n = g[0].tasks.len()
            var i = 0
            while i < n {
                let st = __saw_exec_slot_state(g, i)
                if (not st.done) && (not st.active) {
                    anylive = true
                    if __park_is_io(st.remaining) {
                        io = true
                        let dl = g[0].tasks.with_var_ref<Int>(i, { f in
                            f!.io_deadline()
                        })
                        deadline = __park_deadline_fold(deadline, dl, now)
                    } else if __park_is_flag(st.remaining) {
                        flag = true
                    } else if st.remaining > 0 {
                        if deadline < 0 || st.remaining < deadline {
                            deadline = st.remaining
                        }
                    }
                }
                i = i + 1
            }
            cur2 = g[0].next
        }
        if not anylive {
            // Every live frame in the list is active on an outer sweep's stack:
            // cede to it (reentrancy: never spin, never re-enter a live
            // coroutine).
            return
        }
        // A frame parked on a readiness word can be woken by a thread this
        // sweep is not watching, so whatever it parks in below must be bounded
        // whenever one exists. `__saw_exec_flag_bound` is that cap applied to a
        // deadline; the promote at the top of phase 1 reads the word when the
        // park returns.
        if io {
            if __saw_exec_any_latched_io() || __saw_exec_any_cancelled_io() {
                // A parked frame's event was already latched (consumed by
                // another poll; see `__saw_exec_any_latched_io`) or a peer
                // cancelled it through `cancel_addr` (no reactor wake): wake it
                // now instead of blocking in a poll on an event that will not
                // come.
                __saw_exec_wake_io(1, deadline, now)
            } else {
                // The advance passed to `__saw_exec_wake_io` is what the poll
                // actually waited, not what the sleepers asked for: a poll cut
                // short by the flag bound has not earned the whole deadline.
                let tmo = __saw_exec_flag_bound(deadline, flag)
                let events = __saw_host_reactor().poll(__saw_exec_poll_ms(tmo))
                // The promote is judged against the clock after the poll, not
                // the pre-poll `now` the bound was computed from: the poll is
                // where the time a deadline measures goes by.
                __saw_exec_wake_io(events, tmo,
                                   __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() as Int)
            }
        } else if flag && deadline < 0 {
            // Nothing but readiness-word parks. Nothing on this thread can
            // publish one (a cooperative publisher would have been runnable,
            // and phase 1 would have run it), so the wake comes from another
            // thread, or this is a deadlock (the quiescent report is checked
            // here). Park in the reactor so a `__saw_exec_chan_wake` poke ends
            // the wait at once, bounded so a poke that races the poll cannot be
            // lost.
            __saw_exec_check_quiescent(false)
            let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(__saw_exec_poll_ms(FLAG_POLL_NANOS))
        } else if deadline > 0 {
            // Timer idle: every live frame is sleeping or on a readiness word,
            // and none is on an fd. Park in the reactor with the earliest
            // deadline as the poll timeout rather than in a plain sleep. No
            // frame of this sweep is registered, so for this sweep the poll is
            // a timer, but a pokeable one: the reactor wake reaches it where a
            // plain sleep could not, so a cancel arriving during a long nap is
            // observed at the poke instead of at the deadline.
            if not __saw_exec_wake_cancelled_sleepers() {
                let before = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos()
                let _ = __saw_host_reactor().poll(
                    __saw_exec_poll_ms(__saw_exec_flag_bound(deadline, flag)))
                let spent = __saw_rt_clock_monotonic_nanos() - before
                // Charge what actually passed, not what was asked for. A poll cut
                // short by a reactor wake has not earned the whole span, and
                // crediting it anyway would wake every sleeper early. Capped at
                // the deadline so the earliest sleeper lands on zero; if the
                // clock reads zero or backward the whole deadline is charged, so
                // the sweep always advances.
                var advance = deadline
                if spent > (0 as Int64) && spent < (deadline as Int64) {
                    advance = spent as Int
                }
                __saw_exec_advance_sleep(advance)
                let _ = __saw_exec_wake_cancelled_sleepers()
            }
        }
    }
}

// The entry executor for a suspending `main` that uses the scheduler. main's
// frame is boxed erased and enqueued as the root member of a scope that lives
// for the whole program; the ambient sweep then drives main and every task it
// spawns (across every group) to completion. `root`'s Deinit at return joins
// any straggler and drops main's box exactly once.
func __saw_exec_run_root(rootbox: Box<any Resumable>) {
    var root = TaskGroup()
    // A `Void` `main` has no handle and no joinable result, so its cell is the
    // empty one: every occupied slot carries a cell, with no special case for
    // the root.
    let _ = root.__enqueue(move rootbox, Box<any __TaskCell>.make(__VoidCell(__cancel: false, __fated: false, __detached: false)))
    __saw_exec_run(0, -1, 0)
}

// The same entry for a `main` that returns something. The value is the
// process exit status and is already an `Int` by the time it reaches the cell
// (the synthesized executor converts it on the way into the frame's result
// slot), so there is one non-void root entry rather than one per `main` shape,
// and this file never has to name a user type.
//
// The caller builds the cell because the frame needs its address at
// construction, before there is anything to hand a root entry; `cellp` is the
// same raw pointer into `cellbox`'s payload that `__spawn_*` takes, and reading
// it here keeps the read inside the group's lifetime: `root`'s Deinit at return
// drops the cell box, and after that the pointer addresses nothing.
//
// `?? 0` is unreachable in practice (the root ran to completion, so its slot
// is occupied) and is the answer for the shape where it did not: a `main` that
// never produced a value did not fail either.
func __saw_exec_run_root_status(rootbox: Box<any Resumable>,
                                cellbox: Box<any __TaskCell>,
                                cellp: UnsafePointer<__ResultCell<Int>>) unsafe -> Int {
    var root = TaskGroup()
    let _ = root.__enqueue(move rootbox, move cellbox)
    __saw_exec_run(0, -1, 0)
    cellp[0].__result.take() ?? 0
}

// =============================================================================
// The background task group (design 242)
// =============================================================================
// `Task.spawn(work(3))` spawns into one process-wide group that nothing
// declares and nothing owns. Two things follow from that:
//
//   * It is lazy. The group is built on the first `Task.spawn` and never
//     before, with the same one static word and create-then-CAS publish as
//     `__saw_host_reactor` above, so a loser destroys its spare and uses the
//     winner's. A program that never spawns a background task pays one zero
//     word in .bss.
//   * Its storage is raw. A `TaskGroup` is `NoMove` because it is a scope
//     whose address its members hold, so there is no way to build one and then
//     put it somewhere: no `Box.make`, no assignment through a pointer, and no
//     const initializer either, since `Vector`'s fields belong to `std.vector`.
//     But an all-zero `TaskGroup` is already a valid empty single-threaded
//     group: every `Vector` field is `{None, 0, 0}`, `workers` is 0 (the ST
//     engine, `< 2`), the lock/cond are absent and every flag is clear. So the
//     getter allocates the bytes, zeroes them, and the group exists. It is
//     never freed: process-lifetime, like the reactor instance.
//
// The exit half is `__saw_bg_close`, which the coroutine transform calls from
// the synthesized `main` of any program that has a background spawn.
static __saw_bg_addr: Atomic<Int> = Atomic(0)

// The process-wide background group, created on first use.
func __saw_bg_group() unsafe -> UnsafePointer<TaskGroup> {
    let cur = __saw_bg_addr.load()
    if cur != 0 {
        return cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    }
    let size = sizeof<TaskGroup>()
    let align = alignof<TaskGroup>()
    if let raw = GlobalAllocator().alloc(size, align) {
        var i = 0
        while i < size {
            raw[i] = 0
            i = i + 1
        }
        let addr = raw as Int
        if __saw_bg_addr.compare_exchange(0, addr) {
            return addr as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        }
        // A peer won the publish. Give the spare back and use the winner's;
        // nothing was enqueued into ours, so there is nothing to tear down.
        GlobalAllocator().dealloc(raw, size, align)
        return __saw_bg_addr.load() as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    }
    // `Task.spawn` is a language form, not a call, so there is no expression a
    // `try` could sit on and nothing to report through. The panic names the
    // operation the author wrote.
    panic("Task.spawn: the background task group could not be allocated")
}

// The program is finished, so every background member is cancelled, then
// joined, then released. Cancel-then-join rather than join alone, because a
// background task is exactly the kind that loops forever and nobody is left to
// ask it to stop; join-after-cancel rather than abandonment, because a task's
// own values deinit when the task completes and a cancelled task completes
// through its cancel path like any other.
//
// Called from the synthesized `main` of every program that has a background
// spawn, and a no-op (one atomic load) in one that never took the getter.
func __saw_bg_close() unsafe {
    let addr = __saw_bg_addr.load()
    if addr == 0 {
        return
    }
    let g = addr as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
    g[0].__cancel_every_live()
    // The group predicate: drive until no frame of this group is live. A
    // cancelled task that ignores its cancel word still runs to completion,
    // which is the cooperative contract.
    __saw_exec_run(addr, -1, 0)
    g[0].__release_slots()
    g[0].__unregister()
    g[0].__bt_unlink()
}

// =============================================================================
// The logical task backtrace (design 158)
// =============================================================================
//
// A suspended task is one allocation: its root coroutine frame, with every
// suspending callee's frame embedded by value inside it, each frame holding a
// `__state` word that names its resume point. The compiler writes down what
// those states mean in the `__saw_bt_table` blob, built by
// sawc/backtrace_table.py, whose docstring is the format's specification: per
// monomorphized frame the byte offset of its `__state` word, and per state
// either the source line the frame is parked on or the embedded child it is
// inside plus that child's offset.
//
// So a task's logical stack is a static table walk from one address. No
// unwinder, no reactor, no allocation, which is what lets this run on the
// panic path, under an allocator that has already refused, and in a
// freestanding image with no heap at all. Every piece of a dump line goes
// straight to the output seam; nothing is assembled into a `String` first,
// because the allocator being gone is precisely when you want the dump.
//
// `tools/lldb_saw.py` decodes the same bytes from outside a stopped process.

extern "C" {
    // The seams, named directly rather than reached through `print`: a dump
    // line is written in pieces (a file, a colon, a number) and `print` ends
    // every call with a newline. `__saw_rt_panic` is the abort policy (hosted
    // it aborts, a kernel decides for itself) and is called last, once the
    // message and the dump are already out.
    func __saw_rt_write(ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, len: Int)
    func __saw_rt_panic(ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, len: Int) -> Never
}

// The table's field offsets, in bytes. The encoder is the other half of this
// contract and these names match the record fields it documents.
static BT_MAGIC: Int = 0x42574153
static BT_HDR_FRAME_COUNT: Int = 8
static BT_HDR_FRAMES: Int = 12
static BT_HDR_STATES: Int = 16
static BT_HDR_STRTAB: Int = 20
static BT_FRAME_REC_SIZE: Int = 24
static BT_FRAME_NAME: Int = 4
static BT_FRAME_FILE: Int = 8
static BT_FRAME_FIRST: Int = 12
static BT_FRAME_STATES: Int = 16
static BT_FRAME_STATE_OFF: Int = 20
static BT_STATE_REC_SIZE: Int = 12
static BT_STATE_CHILD: Int = 4
static BT_STATE_CHILD_OFF: Int = 8

// A logical stack deeper than this, or a group list longer than this, is a
// corrupt structure rather than a program: stop and say so instead of following
// a chain that does not end. The dump may run when something has already gone
// wrong, so every walk here is bounded.
static BT_MAX_DEPTH: Int = 64
static BT_MAX_GROUPS: Int = 1024
// How far the walker looks for a name's terminating NUL: a bound on a bad
// table, not a limit the format imposes.
static BT_MAX_NAME: Int = 256
// Enough digits for any 64-bit value plus room to spare.
static BT_DIGITS: Int = 24

// Reads the little-endian u32 `off` bytes into the table.
func __bt_u32(base: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, off: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    let p = (base + off) as UnsafeConstPointer<UInt32>
    p[0] as Int
}

// Writes a literal to the output seam: no newline, no allocation.
func __bt_emit(text: String) unsafe {
    __saw_rt_write(text, text.len())
}

// Writes a NUL-terminated name out of the table's string table.
func __bt_emit_name(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, off: Int) unsafe {
    if off == 0 {
        __bt_emit("?")
        return
    }
    let base = tbl + __bt_u32(tbl, BT_HDR_STRTAB) + off
    var n = 0
    while n < BT_MAX_NAME {
        if base[n] == 0u8 {
            break
        }
        n = n + 1
    }
    __saw_rt_write(base as UnsafePointer<Int8>, n)
}

// Writes a non-negative integer as decimal digits.
//
// Negative input prints `?` rather than being negated: `Int.min` has no
// positive counterpart, and an overflow trap raised from inside the panic
// reporter is the one failure this path must not have. Nothing here is ever
// negative anyway: lines, slots, generations and counts all start at zero.
func __bt_emit_int(value: Int) unsafe {
    if value < 0 {
        __bt_emit("?")
        return
    }
    var digits: [Int8; BT_DIGITS] = [0; BT_DIGITS]
    var rest = value
    var first = BT_DIGITS
    while true {
        first = first - 1
        digits[first] = ((rest % 10) + 48) as Int8
        rest = rest / 10
        if rest == 0 {
            break
        }
    }
    let p = (&digits) as UnsafePointer<Int8>
    __saw_rt_write(p + first, BT_DIGITS - first)
}

// Is `index` a frame this table describes?
func __bt_known_frame(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    index >= 0 && index < __bt_u32(tbl, BT_HDR_FRAME_COUNT)
}

// The byte offset of frame `index`'s record.
func __bt_frame_rec(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    __bt_u32(tbl, BT_HDR_FRAMES) + index * BT_FRAME_REC_SIZE
}

// The `__state` word of the frame at `addr`, whose type is table entry `index`.
func __bt_state_of(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int, addr: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    let rec = __bt_frame_rec(tbl, index)
    let p = (addr + __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_FRAME_STATE_OFF)) as UnsafePointer<Int>
    p[0]
}

// The byte offset of a state record, or -1 when `index`/`state` name nothing.
// A completed frame holds a state one past the last, which lands here.
func __bt_state_rec(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int, state: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    if not __bt_known_frame(tbl, index) {
        return -1
    }
    let rec = __bt_frame_rec(tbl, index)
    if state < 0 || state >= __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_FRAME_STATES) {
        return -1
    }
    (__bt_u32(tbl, BT_HDR_STATES)
     + (__bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_FRAME_FIRST) + state) * BT_STATE_REC_SIZE)
}

// How many logical frames this task's stack has, the root included.
func __bt_depth(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int, addr: Int) unsafe -> Int {
    var i = index
    var a = addr
    var depth = 0
    while depth < BT_MAX_DEPTH {
        depth = depth + 1
        // Bound the index before reading through it: `__bt_state_of` computes
        // a record offset from it and then dereferences the frame at that
        // offset, so an index the table does not describe is a wild read, and
        // on the panic path a wild read replaces a diagnosable failure with an
        // undiagnosable one. A frame whose `bt_desc` was never patched answers
        // -1, which lands here.
        if not __bt_known_frame(tbl, i) {
            return depth
        }
        let rec = __bt_state_rec(tbl, i, __bt_state_of(tbl, i, a))
        if rec < 0 {
            return depth
        }
        let child = __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_STATE_CHILD)
        if child == 0 {
            return depth
        }
        a = a + __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_STATE_CHILD_OFF)
        // Biased by one in the encoding so a 32-bit walker can read the field
        // into its own `Int`: 0 is "leaf", not "frame 0".
        i = child - 1
    }
    depth
}

// Writes the one logical frame `want` levels below the root (0 = the root).
//
// Re-walking from the root per level is O(depth^2), and deliberately so: the
// depth is bounded at BT_MAX_DEPTH, and it buys a walk with no storage at all,
// which is what makes the dump work with the allocator gone.
func __bt_emit_frame(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int, addr: Int,
                     want: Int) unsafe {
    var i = index
    var a = addr
    var step = 0
    while step < want {
        if not __bt_known_frame(tbl, i) {   // see `__bt_depth`
            return
        }
        let rec = __bt_state_rec(tbl, i, __bt_state_of(tbl, i, a))
        if rec < 0 {
            return
        }
        let child = __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_STATE_CHILD)
        if child == 0 {
            return
        }
        a = a + __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_STATE_CHILD_OFF)
        i = child - 1
        step = step + 1
    }
    if not __bt_known_frame(tbl, i) {
        __bt_emit("    at <unknown frame>\n")
        return
    }
    let rec = __bt_frame_rec(tbl, i)
    let state = __bt_state_rec(tbl, i, __bt_state_of(tbl, i, a))
    __bt_emit("    at ")
    __bt_emit_name(tbl, __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_FRAME_FILE))
    if state >= 0 {
        __bt_emit(":")
        __bt_emit_int(__bt_u32(tbl, state))
    }
    __bt_emit(" in ")
    __bt_emit_name(tbl, __bt_u32(tbl, rec + BT_FRAME_NAME))
    __bt_emit("\n")
}

// Writes one task's whole logical stack, innermost frame first: a reader looks
// for where a task is stopped, not where it started, the usual backtrace
// convention.
func __bt_emit_stack(tbl: UnsafeConstPointer<UInt8>, index: Int, addr: Int) unsafe {
    var level = __bt_depth(tbl, index, addr) - 1
    while level >= 0 {
        __bt_emit_frame(tbl, index, addr, level)
        level = level - 1
    }
}

// Is this slot holding a task that has not finished?
//
// A completed task's frame box is released and its slot retired, so the `done`
// flag is what keeps a recycled slot from being counted: whatever the allocator
// did with those bytes since is not a coroutine frame, and a reused slot gets
// `done = false` again at the same moment it gets a frame.
//
// The test deliberately does not ask whether `tasks` holds a box at that
// index. An MT worker claims a frame by moving the box out of its slot for the
// length of the resume, so an absent box can mean "running on a worker" as well
// as "gone", and a running task is the one state a dump most needs to show
// (the dumping task is itself in it). The walk reads the slot's recorded
// `frame_desc`/`frame_addr` instead, which the enqueue wrote, the completion
// clears, and a claim does not disturb.
func __bt_slot_live(g: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>, slot: Int) unsafe -> Bool {
    not g[0].done.get(slot)!
}

// Why this task is not running, in the executor's own vocabulary.
func __bt_emit_status(g: UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>, slot: Int) unsafe {
    if g[0].active.get(slot)! {
        __bt_emit("running")
        return
    }
    let remaining = g[0].remaining.get(slot)!
    if __park_is_io(remaining) {
        __bt_emit("io-parked")
    } else if __park_is_flag(remaining) {
        // Parked on a readiness word, which only a channel wait produces, and
        // that is what the dump should say to a reader looking for a deadlock.
        __bt_emit("channel-parked")
    } else if remaining == 0 {
        __bt_emit("ready")
    } else {
        __bt_emit("sleeping")
    }
}

// Walk every live task slot across the diagnostics list, bounded to
// BT_MAX_GROUPS, calling `body` with the group pointer, that group's 1-based
// ordinal, and the slot index. `__saw_bt_live_count` and `__saw_bt_dump` share
// it, so they agree on the bound, the live test and the group-then-slot order.
func __bt_each_live_slot(body: (UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>, Int, Int) unsafe -> Void) unsafe {
    var cur = __saw_bt_head.load()
    var number = 0
    while cur != 0 && number < BT_MAX_GROUPS {
        number = number + 1
        let g = cur as UnsafePointer<TaskGroup>
        let n = g[0].tasks.len()
        var i = 0
        while i < n {
            if __bt_slot_live(g, i) {
                body(g, number, i)
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        cur = g[0].bt_next
    }
}

/// How many tasks are live right now, across every group, single-threaded and
/// multi-threaded.
func __saw_bt_live_count() unsafe -> Int {
    var count = 0
    __bt_each_live_slot({ [&var count] g, number, i in
        count = count + 1
    })
    count
}

/// Writes every live task's logical backtrace to the runtime's output seam.
///
/// The slot walk takes no lock. A multi-threaded group's worker may be
/// mid-resume while this reads its frame, so what comes out is a best-effort
/// snapshot, and the header says so.
public func __saw_bt_dump() unsafe {
    let tbl = __saw_bt_table()
    if __bt_u32(tbl, 0) != BT_MAGIC {
        return
    }
    let live = __saw_bt_live_count()
    if live == 0 {
        __bt_emit("saw tasks: none live\n")
        return
    }
    __bt_emit("saw tasks: ")
    __bt_emit_int(live)
    // Say when the snapshot was taken, because that bounds how much to trust
    // it: from the panic path the program is about to end; from a live call it
    // is still moving underneath the walk.
    if __saw_bt_dumping.load() != 0 {
        __bt_emit(" live (as-of panic, unsynchronized)\n")
    } else {
        __bt_emit(" live (unsynchronized snapshot)\n")
    }
    __bt_each_live_slot({ g, number, i in
        __bt_emit("  task group ")
        __bt_emit_int(number)
        __bt_emit(" slot ")
        __bt_emit_int(i)
        __bt_emit(" gen ")
        __bt_emit_int(g[0].gen.get(i)!)
        __bt_emit(" ")
        __bt_emit_status(g, i)
        __bt_emit("\n")
        // The frame's type and address, recorded at enqueue rather than read
        // out of the box here: the box may be out of its slot, owned by
        // whichever worker is resuming it, and a torn read of a two-word box
        // handle from an unsynchronized walk is a wild pointer.
        let addr = g[0].frame_addr.get(i)!
        if addr != 0 {
            __bt_emit_stack(tbl, g[0].frame_desc.get(i)!, addr)
        }
    })
}

/// The panic sink for a program that links the executor.
///
/// Writes the panic line, then the task dump, then hands the abort to the
/// runtime seam with nothing left to say. A panic with no live task takes the
/// seam directly with its message, so it prints what it would print in a
/// program without the executor.
///
/// One flag guards re-entry: a panic raised by the walk (a bounds check, a
/// half-written frame) reports itself through the plain seam instead of
/// re-entering the walk that raised it.
func __saw_bt_panic(message: UnsafePointer<Int8>, length: Int) unsafe -> Never {
    if __saw_bt_dumping.load() != 0 {
        __saw_rt_panic(message, length)
    }
    __saw_bt_dumping.store(1)
    if __saw_bt_live_count() == 0 {
        __saw_rt_panic(message, length)
    }
    __saw_rt_write(message, length)
    __saw_bt_dump()
    __saw_rt_panic(message, 0)
}
